(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["correction-log-2026-10-09"]={"html":"<h1>Correction log — 9 October 2026 (v1.3.3)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.3 to text signed at v1.3.2. This version corrects citations and statements against their sources. It states the opinion and tiering rules once and makes them agree. It scores Recoverability on the scale's own levels and cites PRA supervisory statements as their current versions. It adds published methods to research bases, adds rows 47 to 91 to the incident register and cites them from the canvases. It changes the grading in two test procedures, opens two under-research observations and adds an AI trust view. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-03.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source; the other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. A sentence that names a row or a line and the kind of change declares that change; the row carries the new values. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.3 (2026-10-09) lines, which give the detail for each canvas.</p>\n<p>No ★ rating changes. No Independent Review Question or claim is added, removed or reworded, and no procedure's reviewer-day figure changes. Risk profiles change on one axis, Recoverability (section 5). Two test procedures change how a case is graded: TP-CON-001 and TP-RES-007 (sections 10, 11.1 and 11.2). Two under-research observations are opened: URO-7 on IRQ-SEC-003 and URO-8 on IRQ-SEC-001 (section 11.3). URO-6 passed its signing-batch limit unresolved and stays open to its date bound (section 11.7). The release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2, \"a version release that changes the standard's substance\". It makes a register addition (incident register rows 47 to 91), changes the grading in two test procedures and opens two observations. Where a canvas's field 18 Research Basis changes in substance, its \"Research basis last reviewed\" date moves to 9 October 2026. A correction to the form of a citation in field 18 moves the date too (for example IRQ-MOD-009). A wording-only edit to field 18 leaves the date where it was (IRQ-MOD-015).</p>\n<h2>1. Citations and sources</h2>\n<h3>1.1 Authors, titles, venues and pinpoints</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chen, Zaharia and Zou (arXiv:2307.09009).</strong> The citation gives its publication in Harvard Data Science Review 6(2), 2024 (IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-DAT-011, -012, IRQ-MOD-002, -010, -013; <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>). IRQ-DAT-011 corrects the first author to Chen, L.</li>\n<li><strong>METR time-horizon paper.</strong> It is cited as Kwa et al. (METR), under its current title, \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks\", with its venue, NeurIPS 2025 (IRQ-AGT-001; the academic register). The arXiv record lists people and no organisation author.</li>\n<li><strong>Authors.</strong> IRQ-GOV-010, reference [3], \"Visibility into AI Agents\": the co-authors are Chan, A., Ezell, C., Kaufmann, M., et al. IRQ-SEC-002: three initials of Abdulhai et al. (arXiv:2510.14318) are corrected (M. Abdulhai, R. Cheng, A. Shrivastava). Constitutional Classifiers is cited as Sharma et al. (Anthropic) (IRQ-SEC-001), \"Auditing Language Models for Hidden Objectives\" as Marks et al. (Anthropic) (IRQ-MOD-009), and the Microsoft red-teaming paper as Bullwinkel et al. (IRQ-AGT-013). Souly et al. is cited with the affiliations the paper gives (UK AI Security Institute, Anthropic, Alan Turing Institute) (IRQ-DAT-006; the academic register). The academic register gives Kalai, Nachum, Vempala and Zhang as authors.</li>\n<li><strong>Titles given as issued, in the canvases and the registers.</strong> Gerstgrasser et al., ending \"Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data\" (IRQ-DAT-004, -013, IRQ-MOD-012). Rabanser et al., \"Failing Loudly: An Empirical Study of Methods for Detecting Dataset Shift\" (IRQ-MOD-010). NIST AI 100-2 E2025, \"Adversarial Machine Learning: A Taxonomy and Terminology of Attacks and Mitigations\" (IRQ-SEC-005). \"Ragas: Automated Evaluation of Retrieval Augmented Generation\" and \"Seven Failure Points When Engineering a Retrieval Augmented Generation System\" (IRQ-DAT-007, -008, -012). \"Jailbreaking Black Box Large Language Models in Twenty Queries\", with PAIR as the method name (IRQ-SEC-001, -002). \"Great, Now Write an Article About That: The Crescendo Multi-Turn LLM Jailbreak Attack\" (IRQ-SEC-002). \"Constitutional Classifiers: Defending against Universal Jailbreaks across Thousands of Hours of Red Teaming\" (IRQ-SEC-001). Willison, \"The lethal trifecta for AI agents: private data, untrusted content, and external communication\", dated 16 June 2025, without the words \"widely cited\" (IRQ-AGT-004). \"Secret Collusion among AI Agents: Multi-Agent Deception via Steganography\" (IRQ-AGT-007). \"Poisoning Attacks on LLMs Require a Near-constant Number of Poison Samples\" (IRQ-DAT-006). \"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Limited LLM Benchmark\" (IRQ-MOD-011). \"Lessons From Red Teaming 100 Generative AI Products\" (IRQ-AGT-013). \"Project Vend: Can Claude run a small shop? (And why does that matter?)\" (IRQ-AGT-008, -010). \"AILuminate: Introducing v1.0 of the AI Risk and Reliability Benchmark from MLCommons\" (IRQ-CON-009). Datta, Sen and Zick, with the subtitle \"Theory and Experiments with Learning Systems\" (IRQ-CON-002). Anderson (2007), \"The Credit Scoring Toolkit: Theory and Practice for Retail Credit Risk Management and Decision Automation\" (IRQ-CON-007). The Microsoft AI Red Team whitepaper, \"Taxonomy of Failure Mode in Agentic AI Systems\", at 13 places (IRQ-AGT-001, -010, -011; TP-AGT-010; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_AGT.md</code>; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT.md</code>). The Seven Failure Points title had a short form, \"… When Engineering a RAG System\", at seven places (IRQ-DAT-007 and IRQ-DAT-008, fields 9, 18 and 20 of each; the academic register). Six of them now give the title in full. IRQ-DAT-007 field 9 no longer cites the paper (section 1.2).</li>\n<li><strong>Register titles.</strong> The academic and labs registers give the issued titles of the papers above and of further rows. Among them are Zou et al. (GCG), \"Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models\", Greshake et al. and Dell'Acqua et al., with its full subtitle. The Microsoft taxonomy whitepaper is cited by its cover title, which reads \"Failure Mode\". The LlamaFirewall row gives its full title and the date May 2025. The academic register also gives the issued titles of Farquhar et al., Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan, Chouldechova (with its subtitle), Prince and Schwarcz, Shokri et al. and Lukas et al. The labs register gives the OpenAI paper on external red teaming as \"OpenAI's Approach to External Red Teaming for AI Models and Systems\". In the academic register, Farquhar et al. now reads \"Detecting hallucinations in large language models using semantic entropy\"; Greshake et al. \"Not what you've signed up for: Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection\"; Dell'Acqua et al. \"Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier: Field Experimental Evidence of the Effects of Artificial Intelligence on Knowledge Worker Productivity and Quality\"; and Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan \"Inherent Trade-Offs in the Fair Determination of Risk Scores\". Chouldechova reads \"Fair Prediction with Disparate Impact: A Study of Bias in Recidivism Prediction Instruments\"; Prince and Schwarcz \"Proxy Discrimination in the Age of Artificial Intelligence and Big Data\"; Shokri et al. \"Membership Inference Attacks against Machine Learning Models\"; and Lukas et al. \"Analyzing Leakage of Personally Identifiable Information in Language Models\".</li>\n<li><strong>FCA FG22/5.</strong> It is cited under its issued title, \"Final non-Handbook Guidance for firms on the Consumer Duty\", in place of a description in quotation marks (IRQ-CON-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -008, -009).</li>\n<li><strong>Bank of England and PRA letter of 22 April 2024.</strong> It is cited by its title, \"The Bank and the PRA's response to DSIT/HMT: update on our approach to AI\", with the Bank of England and the PRA as issuers (IRQ-GOV-001, -004, -007, -008, -012, IRQ-TPR-003; <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>). It had been cited as a PRA or PRA/FCA document under other titles. The register row gives the date 22 April 2024, in place of April 2024. In field 17 of IRQ-GOV-001, -005, -006, -007 and -008, five sentences called it the PRA's April 2024 mapping. They now name the letter.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAI card.</strong> It is cited by its title, Operator System Card, 23 January 2025 (IRQ-AGT-012, -013; <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>). The Computer-Using Agent is the model the card describes.</li>\n<li><strong>Pinpoints.</strong> IRQ-GOV-002: the ISO/IEC 42001 clause for the scope of the AI management system is 4.3, not 4.1 (fields 9, 17 and 20). Field 9 cites clause 8.1, operational planning and control, in place of clause 8. IRQ-SEC-007: the ICO Guidance on AI and data protection is cited at its section \"How should we assess security and data minimisation in AI?\", with that section's URL. The statement that the guidance cannot be pinpointed is removed. The canvas carries the hub notice that the guidance is under review after the Data (Use and Access) Act.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 4600.</strong> The standard takes the title of its current edition, \"Standard for Evaluation of Autonomous Products\" (Edition 3, 17 March 2023), at two places (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>; IRQ-GOV-012, reference [8]).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.2 Statements corrected to what the source says</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-007.</strong> The control for duplicate and superseded document versions no longer cites Barnett et al. and is labelled Emerging Practice. The paper's seven failure points do not include duplicate, superseded or stale documents. Field 18 and the four-test footer describe the failure points the paper names. Field 11 and TP-DAT-007's method line state that the seeding design, a superseded document placed beside its replacement, is the canvas's own, and cite the paper for RAG failure in general.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-008.</strong> Barnett et al. name contradicting information in the context as one cause of failure point FP4 (\"Not Extracted\"). The canvas no longer presents conflicting-document retrieval as a failure point of its own (fields 9, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-010.</strong> Chan et al. assess activity logging (logs of tool use, internal reasoning, self-critique and memory management) as a visibility measure. The paper does not discuss intermediate or abandoned steps.</li>\n<li><strong>The PRA's October 2025 model risk roundtable (IRQ-GOV-007, -012; the regulatory register).</strong> The page, published in November 2025, reports two roundtables, on 20 and 22 October 2025. The slides cover model tiering, development testing, independent validation and monitoring under SS1/23. The statements that the page or the slides address validation of non-deterministic or generative AI models are removed. The register rows no longer say that the page signals the likely direction of SS1/23 updates, and they name the PDF as the PRA's presentation slides, in place of a meeting note. The page row states that the PRA presented its thinking at two chief risk officer roundtables with 21 PRA-regulated firms, that the page was published on 24 November 2025, and that it links the slides only. The human-oversight and agentic domain tags of the two register rows are removed: the slides support neither. IRQ-GOV-007 field 9 describes the slides' content as they give it. IRQ-GOV-012 field 17 narrows the letter of 22 April 2024 to what it says. SS1/23 includes expectations for dynamic models, and the Bank and the PRA keep their approach under continuous review. The page is cited by its heading, \"The PRA holds model risk management roundtable on artificial intelligence and machine learning technologies\", at four places (IRQ-GOV-007 fields 17 and 20, IRQ-GOV-012 field 20 and the title of the register row). The register summary's line on the roundtable reads \"PRA MRM AI and ML roundtable (Oct 2025) presenting the PRA's current thinking on applying SS1/23 to AI and ML\", in place of \"PRA MRM AI roundtable (Oct 2025) signalling how SS1/23 stretches to GenAI\".</li>\n<li><strong>Silent provider-side drift (IRQ-TPR-004).</strong> The [UNVERIFIED] marker on silent drift is removed. Two peer-reviewed studies are cited: Chen, Zaharia and Zou (Harvard Data Science Review 6(2), 2024) and Gao, Liang and Guestrin (ICLR 2025). Cai et al. (2025, workshop poster) is cited for the reported limit of statistical tests on text outputs. References [4] to [6] are added. The URO-3 text, at the canvas and in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, states that UpgradeBench is not a source on silent provider-side change. The URO-3 entry of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> also states that the tag at IRQ-TPR-004 is removed on two peer-reviewed studies, so the resolution of URO-3 does not turn on the tag. IRQ-MOD-002's UpgradeBench note says the same. IRQ-RES-006 names the journal-published study and states what it does not address; its [UNVERIFIED] marker on slow decay stands.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-008.</strong> The Bank of England Financial Stability Report of July 2025 is no longer cited as a source on AI, and its reference entry is removed. The report has no section on AI and points to the April 2025 Financial Stability in Focus, which remains the basis. The register row and the register summary no longer describe the July 2025 report as an AI source. The canvas's change log now says that the report mentions AI only in passing and does not discuss cloud provider concentration (one place).</li>\n<li><strong>Dell'Acqua et al. (the academic register).</strong> The claim follows the abstract: on a task selected to be outside the frontier, consultants using AI were 19% less likely to produce correct solutions. The relevance cell states that the abstract names no cause, so automation bias is not shown by it. The claim cell no longer quotes \"falling asleep at the wheel\", and the relevance cell is headed \"Reliance on LLM output outside the capability frontier\", in place of \"Workforce-level automation bias with LLMs\".</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-009.</strong> The NCSC absence marker is narrowed. NCSC Machine learning principles 2.1, \"Secure your supply chain\" (Version 2.0, published 22 May 2024), is cited as NCSC guidance on the model supply chain, and reference [7] is added. The absence claim is limited to a standalone NCSC publication.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-010.</strong> The tag on supervisory guidance for MCP and tool-server protocols no longer says \"confirmed\" for a negative search. It names the FCA Mills Review (6 July 2026, p.141): MCP and similar protocols lack strong identity guarantees, clear mandate structures and strong audit standards.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-002.</strong> The tag on sector-body publications names the FINOS AI Governance Framework mitigation AIR-PREV-010 (AI Model Version Pinning). The search statement names UK Finance and ISDA only.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-006.</strong> The tag cites non-financial-services peer-reviewed work on retrieval across embedding-model versions (Vejendla, EMNLP 2025) and states that no study specific to financial services was located.</li>\n<li><strong>FS2/23 (IRQ-MOD-012; the regulatory register).</strong> The feedback statement summarises respondents' views and ranks no concerns. The canvas no longer says that FS2/23 identifies data management as a top AI risk concern. It gives two of the key points that respondents made. The register row no longer ranks concerns either: it records the 54 responses, states that the feedback statement contains no policy proposals, and gives the approach that most respondents suggested (para 2.4) and two of their key points.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>.</strong> Commitment 1 no longer says that the source registers were web-verified. It says that canvases draw on registered sources, in place of pre-verified citations. Each register's notes state how its rows were checked.</li>\n<li><strong>MAS (IRQ-AGT-001, -003, -004, IRQ-TPR-001).</strong> Four statements are narrowed to what MAS says. The consultation paper proposes no control specific to the tools of AI agents (IRQ-AGT-003). Its lifecycle controls are general, and two limbs bear on AI agents (IRQ-AGT-001, -004). The paper names tool access as a risk (paragraph 1.10 of the proposed Guidelines). It proposes that evaluation and testing of AI agents cover their key failure modes (paragraph 4.15), a least-privilege review of the AI system architecture (paragraph 4.22(a)), and monitoring of reasoning processes, actions taken and tools used (paragraph 4.23(a)). The canvases cite the final Guidelines of 7 October 2026 for the same limbs and for what they add: the effectiveness of associated guardrails (paragraph 5.15), tool access as an example inventory attribute (paragraph 4.8, footnote 25) and enhanced documentation for AI agents (paragraph 5.23(d)). IRQ-AGT-001 also cites them for the goal divergence it tests (paragraph 1.11). They also give the dates, at four places: the Guidelines take effect on 7 October 2027, and firms may meet the expectations of Sections 5 and 6 by 7 October 2028 (paragraph 1.8). IRQ-TPR-001 no longer says that firms wait for sector-specific guidance on AI provider diligence. It cites the final Guidelines for the onboarding and use of third-party AI (paragraphs 5.10 and 5.11). The line now says that no published FS-specific safety or security evaluation checklist exists, in place of no single published FS-specific standard: MAS sets areas to consider, each of which may be met to varying degrees, and not a checklist. The MAS reference entries of IRQ-AGT-001, -003 and -004 carry the paper's number, P017-2025, and the dates of the final Guidelines. IRQ-TPR-001 adds reference [8].</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-010.</strong> The quotation of PRIN 2A.6.2R(3) runs to the end of its limb, \"options, including any risks\" (fields 9 and 17; two places).</li>\n<li><strong>Microsoft AI Red Team taxonomy (IRQ-AGT-010, -011; TP-AGT-010).</strong> The taxonomy names no workflow-deviation category, and it does not use the term \"hidden chains\". The two attributions are removed at eight places. IRQ-AGT-010 (fields 9, 11, 18 and 21) and TP-AGT-010's method classification cite the taxonomy's failure mode \"Agent flow manipulation\" (printed p. 18) and its effect \"Agent misalignment\" (printed p. 7). TP-AGT-010's ★ summary keeps its grounding without the category. IRQ-AGT-011 (fields 18 and 21) cites the failure mode \"Insufficient transparency and accountability\" (printed p. 24). \"Hidden chains\" stays as FAIAS's own term. IRQ-AGT-010 field 18 and the labs register's row for \"Lessons From Red Teaming 100 Generative AI Products\" no longer call Microsoft's AI Red Team the largest corporate AI red team. Neither source says so (two places). No ★ rating changes.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-SEC-001.</strong> The override-rate threshold of 0% rests on the canvas pass indicator. OWASP LLM01:2025 gives mitigation strategies and sets no numeric threshold, so the procedure no longer attributes the threshold to it (one place; URO-8).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.3 Regulatory register: status, dates and identifiers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>EU AI Act.</strong> The prohibited-practices Guidelines are C(2025) 5052 final of 29 July 2025, whose content was approved as C(2025) 884 final and published on 4 February 2025. The [UNVERIFIED] tag on the number becomes [VERIFIED-PRIMARY]. The general-purpose AI scope Guidelines are C(2025) 7719 final of 19 November 2025, whose content was approved as C(2025) 5045 final and published on 18 July 2025, with the library URL. The two Guidelines rows give the titles of the Guidelines as issued. The implementation-timeline row records that the proposal to delay some high-risk dates was adopted as Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 of 8 July 2026. The row gives the Official Journal date, 24 July 2026, and the tracker's last update, 31 August 2026. It also states that the tracker does not name the regulation, whose number and date are taken from the consolidated AI Act. The row cites the consolidation as CELEX 02024R1689-20260727. Annex III high-risk obligations apply from 2 December 2027 and Annex I obligations from 2 August 2028. The row's date range is 2024–2031, and it names the tracker it cites, maintained by the Future of Life Institute, as a secondary source that is not an official EU site. The Act's row states that Article 5(1), first subparagraph, points (ba) and (bb), and Article 5(1a) and (1b) apply from 2 December 2026. It also states that Chapters I and II applied from 2 February 2025, apart from those provisions (Article 113, point (a), as amended). The EBA row no longer links the EBA to Article 4, which is AI literacy. It also states that the consolidated AI Act of 27 July 2026 does not name the EBA.</li>\n<li><strong>MAS.</strong> The consultation paper on Guidelines on AI Risk Management carries its reference number, P017-2025 (IRQ-GOV-002, -004; the register row). The statements that the final Guidelines were not yet issued are replaced, including the regulatory register's statement that the AIRG row's status stood after the speech of 11 September 2026. MAS published the response to feedback and the final Guidelines on 7 October 2026. They take effect on 7 October 2027. The register row adds that firms may meet the expectations of Sections 3 to 4 from that date and those of Sections 5 and 6 by 7 October 2028 (paragraph 1.8). The row also states that it remains the consultation paper, and that the final Guidelines are not yet a row of their own. The Type cell of the register row names the final Guidelines as issued on 7 October 2026, in place of \"expected binding-style Guidelines\" (one place). The row gives the consultation's published closing date, 31 January 2026, in place of January 2026 (one place). IRQ-GOV-002 cites the inventory expectation (paragraphs 4.5 to 4.7) and IRQ-GOV-004 the risk materiality assessment (paragraph 4.10) and proportionality (paragraph 2.1). The register summary and the known-ageing note of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> follow. The known-ageing note no longer lists \"unpublished MAS AIRG final guidelines\" among its examples. The register's verification note gives the counts of the earlier check. Of nine [UNVERIFIED] entries, the check confirmed one, corrected one (the MAS reference number) and kept seven with a reason. The note also states that the same pass recorded three items outside the nine (the NIST COSAIS status as confirmed; the ISO/IEC 27090 exact title and FDIS stage, and the OWASP AI Testing Guide v1.0 publication, as corrected). At v1.3.2 the note counted these items among the nine. The ID 18/24 row states that ID 18/24 is the number of the MAS circular to insurers, and that the paper itself carries no reference number. The Project MindForge row names the Phase 1 whitepaper and the three Phase 2 publications, read at the primary. It links the MAS Project MindForge page, gives the dates 2023–2026, and states that the MDDI advisories it names were not read. The Phase 1 whitepaper is \"Emerging Risks and Opportunities of Generative AI for Banks: A Singapore Perspective\" (executive summary released 15 November 2023). The Phase 2 publications are the AI Risk Management Toolkit's Executive Handbook (November 2025), Operationalisation Handbook (January 2026) and Implementation Examples (January 2026).</li>\n<li><strong>CFPB Circular 2023-03.</strong> The CFPB withdrew the circular on 12 May 2025 (90 FR 20084; FR Doc 2025-08286, item 7). The notice says the withdrawal is not necessarily final. The row no longer calls the circular the US conduct anchor for the explainability of AI credit decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>UK.</strong> A dated note is added to the ICO row after the erratum of 12 September 2026. The ICO's plans page gives \"Winter 2026\" for the final Automated Decision Making and Profiling guidance. For that guidance only, the note corrects the erratum's withdrawal of the target. The erratum's limb on the Code of Practice stands. The White Paper row names CP 815 (March 2023) and the government response, CP 1019 (6 February 2024). The Data (Use and Access) Act row gives the commencement provisions of s.80 and Schedule 6. It cites s.142(2)(h) for the commencement of s.80 at Royal Assent for specified purposes, in place of s.142(1)(2)(h). The Bank of England AI Consortium row is read at the four sets of minutes and becomes [VERIFIED-PRIMARY]. Its description says that the minutes record members' concerns, in place of emerging supervisory concerns, and adds the concentration of AI providers. The four sets of minutes are those of May 2025, October 2025, February 2026 and June 2026. The row names model homogeneity and correlated errors across firms, in place of model herding, and two agentic concerns (agentic payments, safe deployment of agentic tools).</li>\n<li><strong>Standards.</strong> ISO/IEC 27090 is an International Standard under publication (stage 60.00), with the publication date 2026-10. ISO/IEC TS 4213:2022 is current and is expected to be replaced by ISO/IEC DIS 4213. EN 18286:2026 is published, by the CEN-CENELEC news item of 31 July 2026; the Official Journal reference is not checked. The rows give the titles of ISO/IEC 27090 and EN 18286:2026, and the TS 4213 row gives the date October 2022, in place of 2022. The EN 18286 row also cites the CEN-CENELEC newsletter item of 29 July 2026, which says that the Commission is expected to publish the reference in the Official Journal later in 2026. The ISO/IEC 27090 row also records the edition that the ISO page shows, Edition 1.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST.</strong> The COSAIS concept paper was released on 14 August 2025, not 26 August, and an annotated outline followed on 8 January 2026. NIST AI 300-1 (ipd) carries its title; the draft asks for input by 16 September 2026, and the row states that the 13 and 15 October dates are not in the draft (15 October 2026 is the comment date of SP 1353). NIST SP 1353 carries \"Using Artificial Intelligence (AI)\" in its title, in place of \"Using AI\". The NIST AI 300-1 row dates the draft July 2026 and states that 16 September 2026 is a date now passed. It also states that the draft says later input may still be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Other rows.</strong> SB 813 (California): the duty-holder is cited at §§8898.1–8898.2; §8898.3 places duties on a designated IVO. IOSCO: OR/07/2026 is a toolkit extract and not a consultation, and the report builds on two earlier IOSCO AI reports. The row dates the extract May 2026 and states that the report includes a survey that was open until 26 June 2026. The row names the two earlier reports, FR06/2021 and CR/01/2025, as the media release IOSCO/MR/13/2026 does. Brazil, PL 2338/2023: the text before the Chamber of Deputies (Art. 14) does not name credit scoring. The Senate and Chamber steps are read at the primary, and \"modelled partly on the EU AI Act\" is marked as secondary. The row dates the Senate approval 10 December 2024 and the referral to the Chamber 17 March 2025, and states that the bill awaits the rapporteur's opinion in the Special Commission. The row cites Art. 14 as items I to XII, and states that item IV covers access to essential private and public services. The row gives the URLs of the Senate page, the Chamber page and the text, in place of the Senate's host name (one place). China: CAC Order No. 15 is dated 10 July 2023 and in force on 15 August 2023, read at the primary. Korea: the PIPC guide was released on 6 August 2025, read at the primary. The row links the guide page (posted 22 August 2025) and its press release, in place of the PIPC home page, and names the last stage \"application and management\". It gives the guide's Korean title and states that the English title is a translation. Colorado: the row quotes the issuer's description of SB 24-205 and SB 26-189. It no longer calls the rules the first comprehensive US state ADMT rules. ECB: the SSM priorities row gives two priorities and an outlined strategy on AI, with the dated URL. Its quotation reads \"digital and, in particular, AI-related strategies\", in place of \"digital (and AI-related) strategies\". The ESAs DORA incidents report and the HKMA circular are given under their issued titles. The ESAs row describes the report as the first annual overview, the 2025 report, and dates its press release 3 June 2026. The EBA follow-up report on machine learning for IRB models carries its identifier, EBA/REP/2023/28. The EU transparency Guidelines pinpoint reads printed p.49 (PDF page 50).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.4 Labs register and verification record</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Labs register rows.</strong> The Responsible Scaling Policy row lists versions v1.0 to v3.4 with their effective dates, and the date on which the page was last updated, 14 August 2026. Its title reads \"(v1.0 to v3.4)\", in place of \"(v2.x updates)\". It gives each version's effective date (v1.0 19 September 2023; v2.0 15 October 2024; v2.1 31 March 2025; v2.2 14 May 2025; v3.0 24 February 2026; v3.1 2 April 2026; v3.2 29 April 2026; v3.3 26 May 2026; v3.4 8 July 2026, current), in place of \"updated Oct 2024–2025\". The agentic misalignment follow-up row describes four further simulated case studies. It names them (covert code sabotage, assisting fraud, motivated mislabeling and coaching human proxies to whistleblow) and says that one sentence of the post notes mitigation progress on the original blackmail evaluations, in place of saying that the post tracks whether mitigations reduced misaligned agentic behaviour. The GPT-6 Astra System Card row lists its amendments of 9, 22 and 29 September 2026. It counts six change-log entries, in place of two, and adds those of 22 September (a correction of the HealthBench values, an Appendix on GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna and a note on updated alignment evaluations) and 29 September (an Appendix on dots). The o1 System Card row states that the card was updated on 5 December 2024, after the o1-preview card, and gives the reported rates (oversight deactivation in 5%, weight exfiltration in 2% of cases). It gives the date of the o1-preview System Card, 12 September 2024, and no longer calls the o1 card the first reasoning-model card. The Amazon framework row records its update of 17 September 2026, and the zero data retention row records the update of 22 September 2026. By that update, Private Safety Processing is rolling out to API customers, with access expanding in phases, and the row no longer limits its citation to 19 August 2026. The \"Not with a Bug, But with a Sticker\" row describes the book, with its full title and subtitle, authors, publisher, ISBN and date (May 2023), links to the publisher's page in place of MITRE ATLAS, and states that MITRE ATLAS is a separate source. The Mistral moderation row describes the hosted API. It gives the API's 9 categories and two endpoints (raw text and conversational), states that the same API powers moderation in Le Chat, and no longer says that the classifier is for deployers of open-weight models. The Cohere row names Cohere Labs and gives Verga et al. as its example. Verga et al. is cited as \"Replacing Judges with Juries: Evaluating LLM Generations with a Panel of Diverse Models\" (Cohere, 29 April 2024), in place of \"LLMs as auto-graders\" critiques. The Gemini row cites the Gemini 3 Pro Frontier Safety Framework Report, dated November 2025, in place of the Gemini 2.5 and Gemini 3 Pro reports, dated 2025. Four rows link to the pages that carry their sources (OpenAI's practices paper, Anthropic's Frontier Red Team, Purple Llama, AWS AI Service Cards). The AWS row is named \"Nova AI Service Cards / responsible AI resources\", in place of \"Nova model cards / responsible AI evaluations\". The Purple Llama row links Meta's announcement of 7 December 2023 and names its successor page. The links of the OpenAI, Anthropic and AWS rows are <code>https://openai.com/index/practices-for-governing-agentic-ai-systems/</code>, <code>https://www.anthropic.com/research/team/frontier-red-team</code> and <code>https://aws.amazon.com/ai/responsible-ai/resources/</code>, in place of <code>https://openai.com/research/practices-for-governing-agentic-ai-systems</code>, <code>https://red.anthropic.com/</code> and <code>https://aws.amazon.com/ai/responsible-ai/</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code>.</strong> The counts table is corrected to the rows. A note counts a row with two resolutions under the first word of its resolution, in place of the resolution that is dominant. It names the six rows that start CONFIRMED and also carry UNVERIFIED (rows 10, 12, 13, 14, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_B.md</code>.</strong> Two lines carry a dated correction: the NIST COSAIS release date, and the reference numbers of the prohibited-practices Guidelines in row 3 of the regulatory register table. Each correction gives the date or numbers of section 1.3, and the July 2026 results stay as written. A third dated correction, under the summary counts, states that the Corrected column sums to 13, not 12, and gives the count of each section's rows by their resolution; the July 2026 counts stay as written (one place).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/QA_LOG_BATCH1.md</code>.</strong> A dated note follows the quotation of the academic register's Willison entry (one place). It states that the entry now reads \"blog\" and that \"widely cited\" was removed in this version.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. The opinion layer: assurance level, review confidence and evidence</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Assurance level at this version.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Layer 2) states what the register's ratings support. Two tests are rated ★4, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, and both support CLM-09 only. A Tier 2 or Tier 3 opinion, whose scope covers all nine domains, is therefore given at limited assurance in practice. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> refer to that statement, and the template marks its ★ values as illustrative.</li>\n<li><strong>Review confidence.</strong> The rule is stated once, in <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Final Layer), on the ★1–★5 scale, on which no test is rated ★5. High needs a mean ★ of 3.5 or above and L4–L5 evidence above 70%. Medium needs a mean ★ of 2.5 or above and L3–L5 evidence above 70%. Every other case is Low, including an engagement that runs no test. The mean ★ is taken over the tests run in the engagement, each at the rating its canvas carries, in place of a mean across all questions. The indicator table and the worked example follow the rule. The note under the rule states that High review confidence is reachable only by a scope built around the tests rated ★4, which no tier prescribes. The High wording of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part G) states that no tier-prescribed scope reaches the High band, and no longer cites ★4 ratings. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> no longer state that no engagement reaches High. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part G) refer to the rule and no longer restate other thresholds. <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> shows Medium in its example rows. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 states that the review-confidence mean uses each question's current rating and is not a comparison of ratings across anchors. The current rating is the one published in the version of the standard that the engagement applies.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence levels.</strong> <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> defines L1 to L5 as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §5 does, and <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code> applies the scale by reference to §5. In its model card entry, L4 covers evaluation results produced independently of the developer, in place of results from a published benchmark. In its model evaluation entry, L3 is demonstration, in place of design review, and both entries cite §5. The glossary of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (J.1) states that §5 governs. Independent testing in a staged environment is L4; the maturity of its method is rated on the ★ scale. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> refers to the tier's evidence minimum.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier 3 evidence.</strong> L5 before the opinion comes from a production-like system or a similar deployment. Where no such L5 exists for a critical claim, the opinion states the gap and is given at limited assurance. Post-deployment operational validation supplies L5 for the first re-assurance (<code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_RES.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusions and gates.</strong> \"Should Proceed\" rests on evidence that meets the tier's evidence minimum (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>). A Not Testable presumption leaves the claim unassessed. For a Critical claim, the presumptive opinion is then Unable to Form Opinion, as <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code> provides where the evidence is insufficient to assess a Critical claim; this replaces Unable to Form Opinion \"on that claim\". The Deployment Opinion phase includes \"Unable to Form Opinion\", and the Major Change Review phase publishes an updated opinion with a new basis date. Gate 4 of <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> now allows a priority question to be scoped out with rationale, requires no test at Tier 0, rates each test that is run, and states the evidence condition from Tier 2: no material claim rests solely on L1–L2 evidence. The core claims are the claims in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>. The templates' instructions take the core claims from that register, and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> asks for each by its register ID and text. The two templates no longer set a range of 5 to 10 core claims (<code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, Part A.1; <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>, Component 3, Part A); the sub-claims of the safety case (Component 6) keep their range.</li>\n<li><strong>★4 form of words.</strong> <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §8 and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (J.4) no longer say that a ★4 rating is stated without additional caveat (two places). The rating needs no maturity caveat, but a caveat that the test's own canvas requires is still stated. Each ★4 canvas, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, requires one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Tiering, re-assessment and the lifecycle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tier derivation (<code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>).</strong> The score moves in steps of 0.125, and the band table gives each value one tier: 1.000–1.500, 1.625–2.250, 2.375–3.125 and 3.250–4.000. Tier 0 applies only to a system with all five Tier 0 characteristics; a system that scores in the Tier 0 range without them is assigned Tier 1. The tier profiles describe typical systems, and the algorithm assigns the tier.</li>\n<li><strong>Scope.</strong> The Tier 0 profile names its questions, as the Tier 0 scope table does, in place of \"IRQ-GOV series\": IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -005, -009 and -014, at documentary depth. The Tier 1 profile and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5 refer to the Tier 1 scope table. The table takes all GOV questions and priority questions from the Data, Model and Human Oversight domains. It also takes the Resilience and Conduct procedures that the playbook lists for Tier 1. Its critical claims take \"Output confidence is calibrated\" from the list that the profile gave at v1.3.2; the table already held a claim on escalation (\"Escalation pathways work\") and a claim on clean data (\"Data is clean\"), the subjects of that list's two other claims.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-assessment.</strong> Periodic Assurance follows the depth set for each tier (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>). In <code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>, each tier's profile and scope table now give the same re-assessment triggers. The Tier 0 and Tier 1 profiles take the triggers of their tables. Tier 2 adds changed decision logic and new customer segment to its table, and customer segment expansion and operating envelope change to its profile. Tier 3 adds decision logic change and regulatory feedback to its profile, and its table names model change and data change, in place of model version change and training data change. The major-change triggers of <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> apply at every tier, the questions they name are the minimum re-tested within the engagement's scope, and trigger 8, Regulatory Change, is added. <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part H) follows: it requires re-assessment at least of the questions named for the trigger, in place of a full re-assessment. An opinion is valid no later than the next required re-assessment, and annual re-assessment is required at minimum. <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part A.2) states that annual re-assessment is required at minimum, in place of \"recommended\".</li>\n<li><strong>Lifecycle.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> (Phase 6) uses the eight phases of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Layer 4).</li>\n<li><strong>Prioritisation and disclosure.</strong> Phase 3 ranks failure modes by the risk profile, not by a sum or difference of scores, as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §7 forms no composite score. It also notes that on the five-axis scale Detectability 4 is the hardest to detect, a silent failure. <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3c requires a conclusion that rests on a test rated ★3 or below to state the rating and its consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>Versioning and inclusion.</strong> A new subdomain needs maintainer approval and a minor version (<code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §6). <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3a states that a four-test criterion is satisfied when it holds for the deployments to which the question applies. A limit on an answer is stated with the answer and does not make the criterion unmet. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> applies that rule to the four-test records of IRQ-SEC-012 and IRQ-TPR-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Templates and records.</strong> <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> uses questions in its examples that match the claims, and Component 4 lists the seven categories of <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code>. In Component 3, the claim on authorised sources rests on IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-SEC-008 and IRQ-SEC-011, and the claim on escalation rests on IRQ-HUM-004 and IRQ-HUM-005. The two worked examples cite IRQ-MOD-006 (confidence calibration), IRQ-MOD-001 and IRQ-HUM-004, in place of IRQ-MOD-004 (hallucination detection), IRQ-MOD-015 and IRQ-HUM-007. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> states the restructure additions as seven added, net +3 after four merges.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Statements of absence corrected</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-008.</strong> The statement that no peer-reviewed literature addresses operational-memory poisoning is corrected. AgentPoison (NeurIPS 2024) is cited with its limits: it measures attack success, not the validation of memory before reuse. OWASP Agentic AI: Threats and Mitigations (February 2025) is no longer called the first structured articulation of memory poisoning, because AgentPoison (July 2024) is earlier (fields 9 and 17).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-014 and TP-MOD-014.</strong> The statement that no published methodology exists is replaced. Two workshop papers publish repeat-run methods (Atıl et al., 2025; Khatchadourian and Franco, 2025), with their limits. No method specific to financial services was located for the other limbs. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §8 no longer lists TP-MOD-014 as \"None established\". IRQ-MOD-014 field 11 and TP-MOD-014's ★ summary say that test design rests largely on tester judgement, in place of entirely.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-012 and TP-SEC-012.</strong> FinSafetyBench (Findings of ACL 2026) is cited as partial evidence on the refusal limb only. The CFPB report is described as the only verified regulatory anchor. Incident register row 85 is cited as an instance of the mechanism outside financial services, and the gap for financial services is kept (fields 15, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-007.</strong> King et al. (AIES 2025) narrows the [UNVERIFIED] tag on the consumer-versus-enterprise terms pattern. The tag stays for a firm's own contract terms and deployed tier.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-007.</strong> Kozodoi et al. (European Journal of Operational Research, 2025) is cited as a limit on the reject-inference component.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-012.</strong> Roychowdhury et al. (2024) is cited as a published critique of the RAGAS relevance metrics, so a relevance score is read as a comparator. Reference [6] is added.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. Recoverability scores</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Field 16.</strong> Recoverability is scored 3 in place of 2, with the controls-in-place case of field 15 (IRQ-AGT-001, -005, IRQ-CON-001, -002, -008, -009, IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-HUM-001, -002, -003, -004, -006, -007, -008, -009, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-SEC-001, -002, -013, -014, IRQ-TPR-001). Each canvas states recovery within days or longer, except IRQ-SEC-001, which states recovery within hours to days; there the longer period governs. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §7 scores recovery within hours 2 and recovery within days 3. Where a canvas states weeks or months, 3 is the nearest anchor, because the scale names no band between days and years. IRQ-HUM-004's justification says that the irreversible cases pull towards 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Controls-absent cases (field 15).</strong> IRQ-CON-008 and IRQ-CON-009 score 3 in place of 2, so that the case is not lower than the controls-in-place figure. IRQ-HUM-009, IRQ-SEC-009 and IRQ-MOD-006 (IRQ-MOD-006 in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>) score 4 in place of 3, as §7 scores years-long recovery 4.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>.</strong> The field 15 and field 16 examples follow the same levels. No other axis and no ★ rating changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Lines not changed in this version.</strong> Eight controls-in-place lines in field 15 score 2 and state recovery within hours to days, days, weeks, or weeks to months (IRQ-AGT-004, -013, -014, IRQ-CON-005, -006, IRQ-DAT-002, -003, IRQ-TPR-006). In IRQ-GOV-001 the controls-absent line scores 2 and states recovery within days. Each needs a reading of the whole canvas. They go to the next refresh, with the canvases that score 2 and state no period.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. PRA SS1/23 cited as its current version</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The label.</strong> At 65 places in 48 canvases, \"SS1/23, May 2023\" becomes \"SS1/23, April 2026 (16 April 2026, updating 17 May 2023)\" (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, IRQ-DAT-001, -003, -011, -012, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -009, -010, -011, -012, IRQ-MOD-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, -014, -015, -016, IRQ-RES-005, -006, IRQ-TPR-001, -005). The principles the canvases cite are unchanged. The limbs that give the effective date of 17 May 2024 stay, because paragraph 1.5 of the April 2026 version keeps that date. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference entries, other in-text forms and the register note.</strong> The field 20 reference entry for SS1/23 gives the same label and the year 2026 in 59 canvases (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, IRQ-DAT-001, -003, -005, -007, -011, -012, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, IRQ-MOD-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, -014, -015, -016, IRQ-RES-005, -006, IRQ-SEC-003, -004, -006, -008, -009, -013, -014, IRQ-TPR-004). Eight in-text citations in two other forms, \"SS1/23 Model Risk Management, May 2023\" and a quoted title with \"May 2023\", take the label too (IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-SEC-001, -002, -003, -005, -013, -014). IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006 are the two canvases of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>. In IRQ-GOV-008 the entry names LIAF01/26 as the amendment, published 23 April 2026. The currency note at the SS1/23 row of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> now says that the canvases cite the April 2026 version, dated 16 April 2026 on its cover and published and effective 23 April 2026. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>No version given.</strong> Eight in-text citations in field 17 gave no version and take the label. Four name the title, \"PRA SS1/23 Model Risk Management\" (IRQ-SEC-004, -007, -008, -009). Four use a short form, such as \"PRA SS1/23, Principle 3\", in a canvas that has no SS1/23 reference entry (IRQ-DAT-004, -006, -008, IRQ-SEC-010). Each canvas's change log records it. Other short-form citations without a version are not changed, because the same canvas gives the version in another place, such as its field 20 entry. IRQ-CON-007 field 17 states in words that SS1/23 remains the current document (verified 29 July 2026), and it is not changed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>7. Published methods added to research bases</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methods with their limits.</strong> Field 18 cites a published method for the test, or for part of it, and states its limits; field 20 adds the reference. IRQ-AGT-001 (TheAgentCompany), IRQ-AGT-004 (TraceSafe-Bench), IRQ-AGT-005 (SimpleToolHalluBench), IRQ-AGT-007 (Who&amp;When), IRQ-AGT-009 (POSIX), IRQ-AGT-010 (SOP-Bench; token-based replay for conformance checking), IRQ-DAT-003 (inter-coder agreement, Artstein and Poesio; confident learning, Northcutt et al.), IRQ-DAT-004 (Alaa et al.), IRQ-DAT-006 (BackdoorBench, BackdoorLLM and NIST TrojAI, as related work), IRQ-DAT-013 (three studies of manipulated or poisoned feedback, as related work), IRQ-DAT-014 (Jacobs and Wallach, Measurement and Fairness).</li>\n<li><strong>Supervisory and standards sources.</strong> IRQ-CON-008 (FCA, consumer understanding, 13 March 2026), IRQ-GOV-013 (FCA, complaints and root cause analysis), IRQ-GOV-015 (FTC Policy Statement Regarding Advertising Substantiation, 1984), IRQ-GOV-016 (ECB guide on risk data aggregation and risk reporting, May 2024), IRQ-HUM-005 and IRQ-RES-007 (NIST SP 800-84), IRQ-RES-004 (NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, section 3.5), IRQ-SEC-011 (NIST SP 800-53A Rev. 5), IRQ-HUM-004 (ISO/IEC TS 8200:2024, for its published scope only).</li>\n<li><strong>Further peer-reviewed methods.</strong> IRQ-RES-005 (Rabanser, Günnemann and Lipton, NeurIPS 2019), IRQ-RES-006 (Cerqueira et al., KDD 2026), IRQ-SEC-003 (Open-Prompt-Injection, AgentDojo and PoisonedRAG; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>), IRQ-SEC-009 (MalHug, ASE 2024), IRQ-SEC-014 (Pedro et al., ICSE 2025), IRQ-TPR-001 (Foundation Model Transparency Index, 2023 and 2025; NIST SP 1326), IRQ-TPR-005 (Nikolic et al., NeurIPS 2025), IRQ-TPR-006 (MTEB, EACL 2023; Drift-Adapter, EMNLP 2025).</li>\n<li><strong>Related work only.</strong> IRQ-AGT-012 (ST-WebAgentBench) and IRQ-CON-009 (SAGE) cite related work that is not a method for the test. The statements that the canvases keep stand: IRQ-DAT-013's research-gap flag, IRQ-TPR-001's statement on AI provider due diligence and IRQ-TPR-005's [UNVERIFIED] marker. No ★ rating changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. Incident register rows 47 to 91</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>.</strong> Rows 47 to 91 are added, each read at the source its Source cell cites. A note at the foot defines the marker forms. A row whose account rests wholly or in part on another kind of source carries a [secondary — source type] marker. This follows the owner's decision of 6 October 2026. Row 64 carries the marker on the owner's decision of 10 October 2026, because its study is read at its abstract, and IRQ-DAT-014 cites it as a secondary row. The primary kinds are a court, a regulator, an official investigation and the affected organisation's own statement. Of rows 47 to 91, four carry a label in their Incident cell. Rows 62 and 91 are non-AI analogues, row 66 is an audit finding of control gaps, and row 56 is a hard-coded rule in an AI underwriting flow. The Institution cell of 21 rows (61, 65 to 70, 72 to 77 and 81 to 88) states that the sector is not financial services. No IRQ star rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion note and legend.</strong> A dated note at the head (7 October 2026) admits an official audit finding of control gaps with no harm event. The condition is that the audit is evidence for a question about the existence of a control. Row 66 is the first such row. The legend names the foot note of 9 October 2026 on rows 47 to 91.</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>.</strong> The register is described as 91 publicly documented incidents. Four are labelled automation or non-AI analogues (rows 2, 14, 62 and 91), and one is an audit finding of control gaps with no harm event (row 66).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>9. Canvas citations of the new incident rows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Field 18.</strong> 49 canvases cite rows 47 to 91 in the reserved form (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009, -011, IRQ-CON-002, -005, -008, -009, IRQ-DAT-005, -010, -011, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -005, -007, -009, -010, -012, IRQ-HUM-002, -005, IRQ-MOD-001, -005, -011, -014, IRQ-RES-003, -005, -006, IRQ-SEC-002, -003, -005, -008, -009, -010, -011, -014, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009). IRQ-SEC-012's citation of row 85 is recorded in section 4. Each citation states the row's limits: its setting, a secondary marker, or an allegation that is not adjudicated.</li>\n<li><strong>Statements that stand.</strong> Where a row does not meet a field's statement of absence, the statement stands: IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-DAT-013, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-001 and IRQ-TPR-009. IRQ-CON-002's unverified record is narrowed, not cleared, and IRQ-CON-005's data-sparsity limb stays without incident evidence. No ★ rating changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10. Other corrections</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TP-CON-001 §6.</strong> A Fail limb is added for a fairness metric selected, changed or substituted after subgroup or intersectional results have been seen, as IRQ-CON-001 field 13 states it (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON_001.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014.</strong> Field 7 and Test 2 name their criterion: harm to uninvolved third parties is recorded in six of the eleven events (events 1–4, 7 and 10). The AISI quotation ends where AISI's sentence ends. The WHEN line dates the OpenAI / Hugging Face compromise to July 2026 on OpenAI's findings of 26 August 2026, added as reference [15]. The INC-043 exclusion cites the system card at p.55, where the passage stands. No change to ★2, any five-axis value or the count of eleven.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-005.</strong> DORA is cited at Art. 6 (ICT risk management framework) and Art. 9 (protection and prevention), in place of Art. 9 alone. Reference [1] names the latest release of the OWASP Machine Learning Security Top 10, v0.3 (pre-release, 29 September 2023).</li>\n<li><strong>Dated notes.</strong> The notes at IRQ-DAT-014, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-TPR-004 and IRQ-TPR-005 field 17 state what their dates cover.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-001.</strong> The MAS FEAT note names PRIN 2A.2 and PRIN 2A.3 to 2A.6, in place of a \"general fair-treatment outcome\", which PRIN 2A does not contain.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-006.</strong> The Vaccaro et al. title reads \"Does What It Wants\", as the article's first page gives it.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-005 and TP-CON-005.</strong> In the ★1 statement, \"among it\" becomes \"including\".</li>\n<li><strong>EBA/GL/2019/02.</strong> The URL points to the Final Report PDF, in place of a page that now shows the EBA third-party risk management guidelines (IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -003, -007, -009; the regulatory register).</li>\n<li><strong>Related IRQs.</strong> IRQ-MOD-002 and IRQ-MOD-008 list IRQ-HUM-001 in field 19 and in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code>, as their failure chains name it.</li>\n<li><strong>Registers.</strong> The labs register's head note states that a row records a check only where the row gives it. It also states that no row of the register's tables carries an [UNVERIFIED] tag. The labs register row on the August 2026 risk report refers to incident register row 32. The row on the Claude Fable 5.1 and Claude Mythos 5.1 system card gives the model name that each cited page uses. The executive summary (p.4) names Mythos 5.1 and §6.2.2 (p.96) names Fable 5.1, which the card (p.2) describes as the same model with different safeguards. The academic register's ReguSim entry states that the search finds the phrases in that entry only. It also states that the search was repeated on 8 October 2026 with the same result. Incident register row 11 adds CNN, 1 June 2023, which carries the post's wording. A dated correction after the academic register's note of 3 October 2026 states that four further rows, not one, record a Crossref check (one place).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §9.</strong> The index points to the files that hold the full procedure sheets, one file for each procedure, in place of the domain files (one place).</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Live Testing (the regulatory register).</strong> Two lines no longer describe live testing in production as part of FAIAS's evidence model. They state that FAIAS's reviewer never tests production systems and point to the Non-Production Rule in <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 (two places).</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>: the version line, the banner and the errata record.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.3, in place of 1.3.2, and the banner gives its date, 9 October 2026, in place of October 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-09.md</code>, as \"v1.3.3, changes to text signed at v1.3.2\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>: the field 11 example.</strong> The example no longer assumes that the staging environment is realistic to production. It states that this is not assumed without a Parity Declaration, as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> (L3) and <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> state (one place).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>11. Procedures, observations and further corrections</h2>\n<h3>11.1 TP-CON-001 and IRQ-CON-001: the perception component</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Untested component.</strong> This rule applies where no subgroup-labelled evaluation set in the component's modality is available, or where no non-production instance of the component is available. The Pass, Fail or Partial result is then reached without the computation of the component's error rates. The component is reported beside the result as a coverage gap, in the report and in the opinion, in the wording of field 14. It does not by itself make the result a Fail, a Partial or Not Testable. This replaces \"a coverage gap, not a pass\" for the perception component (IRQ-CON-001 fields 11, 12 and 14; TP-CON-001 §3, step 13 and §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Fail limb.</strong> The perception-component Fail indicator also covers the firm's own testing that does not report the component's error rates (field 13; TP-CON-001 §6). The grading of these cases changes. ★3 unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.2 TP-RES-007 and IRQ-RES-007: the scenario library</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fail.</strong> The three named scenario types are provider outage, model behavioural drift and agentic misbehaviour. A scenario library that holds none of them is a Fail, whatever other AI-specific scenarios it holds (field 13; TP-RES-007 §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Partial.</strong> The Partial covers a library that holds the synthetic-media scenario and one or two of those three types. It also covers two further cases, with the gap named. In the first, the AI-specific scenarios were exercised only in an earlier review cycle. In the second, a severity assessment assumes diversification that measured concentration does not support (field 14; TP-RES-007 §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Correlated concentration event.</strong> This scenario type is recorded and reported but not graded. The grading of these cases changes. ★3 · P3 unchanged; the assurance objective is unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.3 URO-7 and URO-8 opened</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-7, IRQ-SEC-003.</strong> OWASP LLM01:2025 gives prevention and mitigation strategies, not a test method: its strategy 7 asks for penetration testing and breach simulations without giving a method. The observation asks whether the ★4 and TP-SEC-003's method class (Established Benchmark) hold once that limb is read as mitigation guidance. Flagged 8 October 2026; resolve by 2027-04-08 (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> fields 11 and 18; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_003.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>URO-8, IRQ-SEC-001.</strong> The observation asks the same question for IRQ-SEC-001, whose independent test names LLM01:2025 as its methodology. It asks whether the ★4 and TP-SEC-001's method class (Established Benchmark) hold once that limb is read as mitigation guidance. The question is whether the other sources that the research basis cites carry the ★4 without it. Flagged 8 October 2026; resolve by 2027-04-08 (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>; IRQ-SEC-001 fields 11 and 18; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_001.md</code>). The method-class table of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> marks the classes of TP-SEC-001 and TP-SEC-003 as under review, with their URO numbers (two places).</li>\n<li><strong>The bound.</strong> §6A.2 states: \"Resolution is bounded: the next signing batch or six months, whichever is first.\" It also states: \"The standing ★ is untouched while a URO is open.\" Both ★4 ratings stand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.4 EU AI Act, Articles 13 and 86</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-008.</strong> Article 13 is cited as the duty from provider to deployer that it is, which gives the customer no right of its own. Article 86(1), the right to explanation of individual decision-making, is added with its scope and its application dates, read in the consolidated text of 27 July 2026. The Act gives a right to an explanation, not a right to contest, and field 5 no longer credits it with contestability expectations. Reference [3] names the articles.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-009.</strong> Article 86 is added and is stated as narrower than the canvas's disclosure control. The person exercises the right for a decision already taken. Annex III point 5(b) excludes systems used to detect financial fraud. Reference [8] is added.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-007.</strong> The Article 13 limb says \"deployers\", the term Article 13(1) uses, in place of \"users\". The Independent Tests and the ★ ratings are unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.5 PRA SS1/21 and SS2/21</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Versions.</strong> SS1/21 is cited as its March 2022 version (11 March 2022, updating 29 March 2021) (IRQ-HUM-003, -005, IRQ-RES-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_RES.md</code>; and IRQ-AGT-014, under \"Text citations\" below). SS2/21 is cited as its November 2024 version (15 November 2024, updating 29 March 2021) (IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -006, -008, -009; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR_009.md</code>, whose change <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> records). In both labels the days are the dates that the PRA's publication page for each statement gives for the current version and for the first publication; the covers give the month. The expectations cited are unchanged. The SS2/21 row of the regulatory register states the currency of the cited passages, including the version published on 18 March 2026 with its renumbered paragraphs. The row also states that the canvases and procedures cite the November 2024 version, in place of the March 2021 text, and what that version changed. That version restated the Solvency II references as PRA Rulebook rules and reworded paragraphs 2.1, 5.3 and 5.4. For the version of March 2026, the row states that it adds notification and register-submission duties for material third party arrangements, extends several Chapter 5 expectations from outsourcing to third party arrangements, and renumbers some cited passages (due diligence from 5.19 to 5.16, concentration from 5.24 to 5.25).</li>\n<li><strong>Reference entries.</strong> The field 20 reference entries give the same versions, with the years 2022 and 2024. SS1/21: IRQ-AGT-002, -005, -006, -012, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-003, -005, IRQ-RES-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007. SS2/21: IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -006, -008, -009. IRQ-GOV-011's entry gives the title as published, \"Outsourcing and third party risk management\". Where an entry also names FCA PS21/3, PS21/3 keeps its own date, 2021. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Text citations.</strong> IRQ-AGT-014 field 17 cites SS1/21 as its March 2022 version and FCA PS21/3 at its own date, 2021, where the citation dated both to 2021. IRQ-GOV-011 fields 9 and 17 give the SS2/21 title as published, \"Outsourcing and third party risk management\".</li>\n<li><strong>Statements corrected to the text.</strong> IRQ-GOV-011: SS2/21 expects firms to keep records of their outsourcing arrangements (para 4.15) and refers to the EBA expectation of a register (para 4.16). The register cross-check is the canvas's extension of those expectations. IRQ-RES-005: SS1/21 states that maintaining operational resilience is a dynamic activity (para 4.16) and states no monitoring expectation. IRQ-RES-006: SS1/21 sets no detection obligation and expects scenario testing to focus on recovery and response (para 6.1). IRQ-RES-003: the limb cites a recoverability expectation, with no containment. IRQ-HUM-005 and IRQ-AGT-002: tested containment is the canvas's reading of the sources, not their requirement; IRQ-HUM-005 field 9 cites PS21/3 for its scenario testing requirement (SYSC 15A.5.3R); IRQ-AGT-002 cites SS1/23 Principle 5.2 for restrictions on model use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.6 Wording, records and citation form</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The maintainer's signing check.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2 and §6A.6 and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> state that the maintainer's signing check enforces the resolve-by bound. <code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code> states that the same check enforces the agreement of each register row's Related-IRQs column with its canvas's field 19. The check is a script that the maintainer runs over the standard's files before each signing. It is not published with the standard. These files name the maintainer's signing check where they said \"the corpus check\", \"enforced mechanically\" or \"machine-checked\". In §6A.6, it is the check that reads a capability-envelope note and that fails a note with no out-of-scope limb.</li>\n<li><strong>Working labels.</strong> The titles of the incident register, the academic register, the labs register, the regulatory register and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> no longer carry phase labels. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_CLAIM_MATRIX.md</code> no longer refer to working phases, and the URO-1 record refers to the maintainer's analysis record (not published). §6A.3 no longer marks the cadence tiers as an assumption. A question once recorded as held for the owner is recorded as an open question (incident register row 34; IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-MOD-002, -009, -015; the academic register).</li>\n<li><strong>CMORG AI Baseline Guidance Review.</strong> It is cited by its cover date, Version 1.0, January 2025, with its publication date, 15 May 2025 (IRQ-RES-001, -007; the regulatory register). The register row states that the served file was created on 14 April 2025, and that an earlier reading recorded May 2025, which the file does not show. The Firm Guidance for Frontier AI row of the regulatory register cites this row as \"AI Baseline Guidance Review, Version 1.0, January 2025\", in place of \"AI Baseline Guidance Review, May 2025\".</li>\n<li><strong>Records.</strong> <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> states that the Control Objective column summarises field 8, which governs. <code>ownership/OWNERSHIP_PROOF.md</code> gives the replaced v1.1.1 signing as dated 26 August 2026 and made on 27 August. The incident register's foot note says that rows 27 to 31 were added on the same day as that signing, in place of the day after. IRQ-SEC-001 gives the number of incidents it cites, two (DPD and Chevrolet of Watsonville), in place of \"repeatedly\". <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code> allows the primary to follow later in the same incident citation and defines <code>YEAR</code> as the year, stated in the row's Date cell, of the event or report on which the citing field relies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.7 URO-6 at its signing-batch limit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-6, IRQ-DAT-009.</strong> This release is a signing batch, so URO-6 passed its signing-batch limit unresolved. It stays open to its date bound of 3 April 2027, and its ★3 stands. A note in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> records this, in the form of the note of 3 October 2026 on URO-2 to URO-5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>12. AI trust view</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TRUST_VIEW.md</code> (new).</strong> The page is an index. It lists the questions of the Governance, Security and Operational Resilience domains and the claims of the Security, Resilience and Governance themes. It adds no question or claim, and no rating changes. It carries no ★ rating or test status. Each row repeats the text of <code>IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> or <code>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>. Where a row and its register differ, the register governs. FAIAS has no \"trust\" domain: under the homing rule, each question keeps its one domain.</li>\n<li><strong>Pointers.</strong> <code>README.md</code> §1 (after the domain table) and §2 (the file map and the reading order), <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TAXONOMY.md</code> (after the homing rule) and <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §1 (after the fundamental posture) point to the page.</li>\n</ul>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>Correction log — 9 October 2026 (v1.3.3)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.3 to text signed at v1.3.2. This version corrects citations and statements against their sources. It states the opinion and tiering rules once and makes them agree. It scores Recoverability on the scale's own levels and cites PRA supervisory statements as their current versions. It adds published methods to research bases, adds rows 47 to 91 to the incident register and cites them from the canvases. It changes the grading in two test procedures, opens two under-research observations and adds an AI trust view. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-03.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source; the other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. A sentence that names a row or a line and the kind of change declares that change; the row carries the new values. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.3 (2026-10-09) lines, which give the detail for each canvas.</p>\n<p>No ★ rating changes. No Independent Review Question or claim is added, removed or reworded, and no procedure's reviewer-day figure changes. Risk profiles change on one axis, Recoverability (section 5). Two test procedures change how a case is graded: TP-CON-001 and TP-RES-007 (sections 10, 11.1 and 11.2). Two under-research observations are opened: URO-7 on IRQ-SEC-003 and URO-8 on IRQ-SEC-001 (section 11.3). URO-6 passed its signing-batch limit unresolved and stays open to its date bound (section 11.7). The release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2, \"a version release that changes the standard's substance\". It makes a register addition (incident register rows 47 to 91), changes the grading in two test procedures and opens two observations. Where a canvas's field 18 Research Basis changes in substance, its \"Research basis last reviewed\" date moves to 9 October 2026. A correction to the form of a citation in field 18 moves the date too (for example IRQ-MOD-009). A wording-only edit to field 18 leaves the date where it was (IRQ-MOD-015).</p>"},{"h":"1. Citations and sources","html":"<h3>1.1 Authors, titles, venues and pinpoints</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chen, Zaharia and Zou (arXiv:2307.09009).</strong> The citation gives its publication in Harvard Data Science Review 6(2), 2024 (IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-DAT-011, -012, IRQ-MOD-002, -010, -013; <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>). IRQ-DAT-011 corrects the first author to Chen, L.</li>\n<li><strong>METR time-horizon paper.</strong> It is cited as Kwa et al. (METR), under its current title, \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks\", with its venue, NeurIPS 2025 (IRQ-AGT-001; the academic register). The arXiv record lists people and no organisation author.</li>\n<li><strong>Authors.</strong> IRQ-GOV-010, reference [3], \"Visibility into AI Agents\": the co-authors are Chan, A., Ezell, C., Kaufmann, M., et al. IRQ-SEC-002: three initials of Abdulhai et al. (arXiv:2510.14318) are corrected (M. Abdulhai, R. Cheng, A. Shrivastava). Constitutional Classifiers is cited as Sharma et al. (Anthropic) (IRQ-SEC-001), \"Auditing Language Models for Hidden Objectives\" as Marks et al. (Anthropic) (IRQ-MOD-009), and the Microsoft red-teaming paper as Bullwinkel et al. (IRQ-AGT-013). Souly et al. is cited with the affiliations the paper gives (UK AI Security Institute, Anthropic, Alan Turing Institute) (IRQ-DAT-006; the academic register). The academic register gives Kalai, Nachum, Vempala and Zhang as authors.</li>\n<li><strong>Titles given as issued, in the canvases and the registers.</strong> Gerstgrasser et al., ending \"Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data\" (IRQ-DAT-004, -013, IRQ-MOD-012). Rabanser et al., \"Failing Loudly: An Empirical Study of Methods for Detecting Dataset Shift\" (IRQ-MOD-010). NIST AI 100-2 E2025, \"Adversarial Machine Learning: A Taxonomy and Terminology of Attacks and Mitigations\" (IRQ-SEC-005). \"Ragas: Automated Evaluation of Retrieval Augmented Generation\" and \"Seven Failure Points When Engineering a Retrieval Augmented Generation System\" (IRQ-DAT-007, -008, -012). \"Jailbreaking Black Box Large Language Models in Twenty Queries\", with PAIR as the method name (IRQ-SEC-001, -002). \"Great, Now Write an Article About That: The Crescendo Multi-Turn LLM Jailbreak Attack\" (IRQ-SEC-002). \"Constitutional Classifiers: Defending against Universal Jailbreaks across Thousands of Hours of Red Teaming\" (IRQ-SEC-001). Willison, \"The lethal trifecta for AI agents: private data, untrusted content, and external communication\", dated 16 June 2025, without the words \"widely cited\" (IRQ-AGT-004). \"Secret Collusion among AI Agents: Multi-Agent Deception via Steganography\" (IRQ-AGT-007). \"Poisoning Attacks on LLMs Require a Near-constant Number of Poison Samples\" (IRQ-DAT-006). \"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Limited LLM Benchmark\" (IRQ-MOD-011). \"Lessons From Red Teaming 100 Generative AI Products\" (IRQ-AGT-013). \"Project Vend: Can Claude run a small shop? (And why does that matter?)\" (IRQ-AGT-008, -010). \"AILuminate: Introducing v1.0 of the AI Risk and Reliability Benchmark from MLCommons\" (IRQ-CON-009). Datta, Sen and Zick, with the subtitle \"Theory and Experiments with Learning Systems\" (IRQ-CON-002). Anderson (2007), \"The Credit Scoring Toolkit: Theory and Practice for Retail Credit Risk Management and Decision Automation\" (IRQ-CON-007). The Microsoft AI Red Team whitepaper, \"Taxonomy of Failure Mode in Agentic AI Systems\", at 13 places (IRQ-AGT-001, -010, -011; TP-AGT-010; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_AGT.md</code>; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT.md</code>). The Seven Failure Points title had a short form, \"… When Engineering a RAG System\", at seven places (IRQ-DAT-007 and IRQ-DAT-008, fields 9, 18 and 20 of each; the academic register). Six of them now give the title in full. IRQ-DAT-007 field 9 no longer cites the paper (section 1.2).</li>\n<li><strong>Register titles.</strong> The academic and labs registers give the issued titles of the papers above and of further rows. Among them are Zou et al. (GCG), \"Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models\", Greshake et al. and Dell'Acqua et al., with its full subtitle. The Microsoft taxonomy whitepaper is cited by its cover title, which reads \"Failure Mode\". The LlamaFirewall row gives its full title and the date May 2025. The academic register also gives the issued titles of Farquhar et al., Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan, Chouldechova (with its subtitle), Prince and Schwarcz, Shokri et al. and Lukas et al. The labs register gives the OpenAI paper on external red teaming as \"OpenAI's Approach to External Red Teaming for AI Models and Systems\". In the academic register, Farquhar et al. now reads \"Detecting hallucinations in large language models using semantic entropy\"; Greshake et al. \"Not what you've signed up for: Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection\"; Dell'Acqua et al. \"Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier: Field Experimental Evidence of the Effects of Artificial Intelligence on Knowledge Worker Productivity and Quality\"; and Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan \"Inherent Trade-Offs in the Fair Determination of Risk Scores\". Chouldechova reads \"Fair Prediction with Disparate Impact: A Study of Bias in Recidivism Prediction Instruments\"; Prince and Schwarcz \"Proxy Discrimination in the Age of Artificial Intelligence and Big Data\"; Shokri et al. \"Membership Inference Attacks against Machine Learning Models\"; and Lukas et al. \"Analyzing Leakage of Personally Identifiable Information in Language Models\".</li>\n<li><strong>FCA FG22/5.</strong> It is cited under its issued title, \"Final non-Handbook Guidance for firms on the Consumer Duty\", in place of a description in quotation marks (IRQ-CON-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -008, -009).</li>\n<li><strong>Bank of England and PRA letter of 22 April 2024.</strong> It is cited by its title, \"The Bank and the PRA's response to DSIT/HMT: update on our approach to AI\", with the Bank of England and the PRA as issuers (IRQ-GOV-001, -004, -007, -008, -012, IRQ-TPR-003; <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>). It had been cited as a PRA or PRA/FCA document under other titles. The register row gives the date 22 April 2024, in place of April 2024. In field 17 of IRQ-GOV-001, -005, -006, -007 and -008, five sentences called it the PRA's April 2024 mapping. They now name the letter.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAI card.</strong> It is cited by its title, Operator System Card, 23 January 2025 (IRQ-AGT-012, -013; <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>). The Computer-Using Agent is the model the card describes.</li>\n<li><strong>Pinpoints.</strong> IRQ-GOV-002: the ISO/IEC 42001 clause for the scope of the AI management system is 4.3, not 4.1 (fields 9, 17 and 20). Field 9 cites clause 8.1, operational planning and control, in place of clause 8. IRQ-SEC-007: the ICO Guidance on AI and data protection is cited at its section \"How should we assess security and data minimisation in AI?\", with that section's URL. The statement that the guidance cannot be pinpointed is removed. The canvas carries the hub notice that the guidance is under review after the Data (Use and Access) Act.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 4600.</strong> The standard takes the title of its current edition, \"Standard for Evaluation of Autonomous Products\" (Edition 3, 17 March 2023), at two places (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>; IRQ-GOV-012, reference [8]).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.2 Statements corrected to what the source says</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-007.</strong> The control for duplicate and superseded document versions no longer cites Barnett et al. and is labelled Emerging Practice. The paper's seven failure points do not include duplicate, superseded or stale documents. Field 18 and the four-test footer describe the failure points the paper names. Field 11 and TP-DAT-007's method line state that the seeding design, a superseded document placed beside its replacement, is the canvas's own, and cite the paper for RAG failure in general.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-008.</strong> Barnett et al. name contradicting information in the context as one cause of failure point FP4 (\"Not Extracted\"). The canvas no longer presents conflicting-document retrieval as a failure point of its own (fields 9, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-010.</strong> Chan et al. assess activity logging (logs of tool use, internal reasoning, self-critique and memory management) as a visibility measure. The paper does not discuss intermediate or abandoned steps.</li>\n<li><strong>The PRA's October 2025 model risk roundtable (IRQ-GOV-007, -012; the regulatory register).</strong> The page, published in November 2025, reports two roundtables, on 20 and 22 October 2025. The slides cover model tiering, development testing, independent validation and monitoring under SS1/23. The statements that the page or the slides address validation of non-deterministic or generative AI models are removed. The register rows no longer say that the page signals the likely direction of SS1/23 updates, and they name the PDF as the PRA's presentation slides, in place of a meeting note. The page row states that the PRA presented its thinking at two chief risk officer roundtables with 21 PRA-regulated firms, that the page was published on 24 November 2025, and that it links the slides only. The human-oversight and agentic domain tags of the two register rows are removed: the slides support neither. IRQ-GOV-007 field 9 describes the slides' content as they give it. IRQ-GOV-012 field 17 narrows the letter of 22 April 2024 to what it says. SS1/23 includes expectations for dynamic models, and the Bank and the PRA keep their approach under continuous review. The page is cited by its heading, \"The PRA holds model risk management roundtable on artificial intelligence and machine learning technologies\", at four places (IRQ-GOV-007 fields 17 and 20, IRQ-GOV-012 field 20 and the title of the register row). The register summary's line on the roundtable reads \"PRA MRM AI and ML roundtable (Oct 2025) presenting the PRA's current thinking on applying SS1/23 to AI and ML\", in place of \"PRA MRM AI roundtable (Oct 2025) signalling how SS1/23 stretches to GenAI\".</li>\n<li><strong>Silent provider-side drift (IRQ-TPR-004).</strong> The [UNVERIFIED] marker on silent drift is removed. Two peer-reviewed studies are cited: Chen, Zaharia and Zou (Harvard Data Science Review 6(2), 2024) and Gao, Liang and Guestrin (ICLR 2025). Cai et al. (2025, workshop poster) is cited for the reported limit of statistical tests on text outputs. References [4] to [6] are added. The URO-3 text, at the canvas and in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, states that UpgradeBench is not a source on silent provider-side change. The URO-3 entry of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> also states that the tag at IRQ-TPR-004 is removed on two peer-reviewed studies, so the resolution of URO-3 does not turn on the tag. IRQ-MOD-002's UpgradeBench note says the same. IRQ-RES-006 names the journal-published study and states what it does not address; its [UNVERIFIED] marker on slow decay stands.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-008.</strong> The Bank of England Financial Stability Report of July 2025 is no longer cited as a source on AI, and its reference entry is removed. The report has no section on AI and points to the April 2025 Financial Stability in Focus, which remains the basis. The register row and the register summary no longer describe the July 2025 report as an AI source. The canvas's change log now says that the report mentions AI only in passing and does not discuss cloud provider concentration (one place).</li>\n<li><strong>Dell'Acqua et al. (the academic register).</strong> The claim follows the abstract: on a task selected to be outside the frontier, consultants using AI were 19% less likely to produce correct solutions. The relevance cell states that the abstract names no cause, so automation bias is not shown by it. The claim cell no longer quotes \"falling asleep at the wheel\", and the relevance cell is headed \"Reliance on LLM output outside the capability frontier\", in place of \"Workforce-level automation bias with LLMs\".</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-009.</strong> The NCSC absence marker is narrowed. NCSC Machine learning principles 2.1, \"Secure your supply chain\" (Version 2.0, published 22 May 2024), is cited as NCSC guidance on the model supply chain, and reference [7] is added. The absence claim is limited to a standalone NCSC publication.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-010.</strong> The tag on supervisory guidance for MCP and tool-server protocols no longer says \"confirmed\" for a negative search. It names the FCA Mills Review (6 July 2026, p.141): MCP and similar protocols lack strong identity guarantees, clear mandate structures and strong audit standards.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-002.</strong> The tag on sector-body publications names the FINOS AI Governance Framework mitigation AIR-PREV-010 (AI Model Version Pinning). The search statement names UK Finance and ISDA only.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-006.</strong> The tag cites non-financial-services peer-reviewed work on retrieval across embedding-model versions (Vejendla, EMNLP 2025) and states that no study specific to financial services was located.</li>\n<li><strong>FS2/23 (IRQ-MOD-012; the regulatory register).</strong> The feedback statement summarises respondents' views and ranks no concerns. The canvas no longer says that FS2/23 identifies data management as a top AI risk concern. It gives two of the key points that respondents made. The register row no longer ranks concerns either: it records the 54 responses, states that the feedback statement contains no policy proposals, and gives the approach that most respondents suggested (para 2.4) and two of their key points.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>.</strong> Commitment 1 no longer says that the source registers were web-verified. It says that canvases draw on registered sources, in place of pre-verified citations. Each register's notes state how its rows were checked.</li>\n<li><strong>MAS (IRQ-AGT-001, -003, -004, IRQ-TPR-001).</strong> Four statements are narrowed to what MAS says. The consultation paper proposes no control specific to the tools of AI agents (IRQ-AGT-003). Its lifecycle controls are general, and two limbs bear on AI agents (IRQ-AGT-001, -004). The paper names tool access as a risk (paragraph 1.10 of the proposed Guidelines). It proposes that evaluation and testing of AI agents cover their key failure modes (paragraph 4.15), a least-privilege review of the AI system architecture (paragraph 4.22(a)), and monitoring of reasoning processes, actions taken and tools used (paragraph 4.23(a)). The canvases cite the final Guidelines of 7 October 2026 for the same limbs and for what they add: the effectiveness of associated guardrails (paragraph 5.15), tool access as an example inventory attribute (paragraph 4.8, footnote 25) and enhanced documentation for AI agents (paragraph 5.23(d)). IRQ-AGT-001 also cites them for the goal divergence it tests (paragraph 1.11). They also give the dates, at four places: the Guidelines take effect on 7 October 2027, and firms may meet the expectations of Sections 5 and 6 by 7 October 2028 (paragraph 1.8). IRQ-TPR-001 no longer says that firms wait for sector-specific guidance on AI provider diligence. It cites the final Guidelines for the onboarding and use of third-party AI (paragraphs 5.10 and 5.11). The line now says that no published FS-specific safety or security evaluation checklist exists, in place of no single published FS-specific standard: MAS sets areas to consider, each of which may be met to varying degrees, and not a checklist. The MAS reference entries of IRQ-AGT-001, -003 and -004 carry the paper's number, P017-2025, and the dates of the final Guidelines. IRQ-TPR-001 adds reference [8].</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-010.</strong> The quotation of PRIN 2A.6.2R(3) runs to the end of its limb, \"options, including any risks\" (fields 9 and 17; two places).</li>\n<li><strong>Microsoft AI Red Team taxonomy (IRQ-AGT-010, -011; TP-AGT-010).</strong> The taxonomy names no workflow-deviation category, and it does not use the term \"hidden chains\". The two attributions are removed at eight places. IRQ-AGT-010 (fields 9, 11, 18 and 21) and TP-AGT-010's method classification cite the taxonomy's failure mode \"Agent flow manipulation\" (printed p. 18) and its effect \"Agent misalignment\" (printed p. 7). TP-AGT-010's ★ summary keeps its grounding without the category. IRQ-AGT-011 (fields 18 and 21) cites the failure mode \"Insufficient transparency and accountability\" (printed p. 24). \"Hidden chains\" stays as FAIAS's own term. IRQ-AGT-010 field 18 and the labs register's row for \"Lessons From Red Teaming 100 Generative AI Products\" no longer call Microsoft's AI Red Team the largest corporate AI red team. Neither source says so (two places). No ★ rating changes.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-SEC-001.</strong> The override-rate threshold of 0% rests on the canvas pass indicator. OWASP LLM01:2025 gives mitigation strategies and sets no numeric threshold, so the procedure no longer attributes the threshold to it (one place; URO-8).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.3 Regulatory register: status, dates and identifiers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>EU AI Act.</strong> The prohibited-practices Guidelines are C(2025) 5052 final of 29 July 2025, whose content was approved as C(2025) 884 final and published on 4 February 2025. The [UNVERIFIED] tag on the number becomes [VERIFIED-PRIMARY]. The general-purpose AI scope Guidelines are C(2025) 7719 final of 19 November 2025, whose content was approved as C(2025) 5045 final and published on 18 July 2025, with the library URL. The two Guidelines rows give the titles of the Guidelines as issued. The implementation-timeline row records that the proposal to delay some high-risk dates was adopted as Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 of 8 July 2026. The row gives the Official Journal date, 24 July 2026, and the tracker's last update, 31 August 2026. It also states that the tracker does not name the regulation, whose number and date are taken from the consolidated AI Act. The row cites the consolidation as CELEX 02024R1689-20260727. Annex III high-risk obligations apply from 2 December 2027 and Annex I obligations from 2 August 2028. The row's date range is 2024–2031, and it names the tracker it cites, maintained by the Future of Life Institute, as a secondary source that is not an official EU site. The Act's row states that Article 5(1), first subparagraph, points (ba) and (bb), and Article 5(1a) and (1b) apply from 2 December 2026. It also states that Chapters I and II applied from 2 February 2025, apart from those provisions (Article 113, point (a), as amended). The EBA row no longer links the EBA to Article 4, which is AI literacy. It also states that the consolidated AI Act of 27 July 2026 does not name the EBA.</li>\n<li><strong>MAS.</strong> The consultation paper on Guidelines on AI Risk Management carries its reference number, P017-2025 (IRQ-GOV-002, -004; the register row). The statements that the final Guidelines were not yet issued are replaced, including the regulatory register's statement that the AIRG row's status stood after the speech of 11 September 2026. MAS published the response to feedback and the final Guidelines on 7 October 2026. They take effect on 7 October 2027. The register row adds that firms may meet the expectations of Sections 3 to 4 from that date and those of Sections 5 and 6 by 7 October 2028 (paragraph 1.8). The row also states that it remains the consultation paper, and that the final Guidelines are not yet a row of their own. The Type cell of the register row names the final Guidelines as issued on 7 October 2026, in place of \"expected binding-style Guidelines\" (one place). The row gives the consultation's published closing date, 31 January 2026, in place of January 2026 (one place). IRQ-GOV-002 cites the inventory expectation (paragraphs 4.5 to 4.7) and IRQ-GOV-004 the risk materiality assessment (paragraph 4.10) and proportionality (paragraph 2.1). The register summary and the known-ageing note of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> follow. The known-ageing note no longer lists \"unpublished MAS AIRG final guidelines\" among its examples. The register's verification note gives the counts of the earlier check. Of nine [UNVERIFIED] entries, the check confirmed one, corrected one (the MAS reference number) and kept seven with a reason. The note also states that the same pass recorded three items outside the nine (the NIST COSAIS status as confirmed; the ISO/IEC 27090 exact title and FDIS stage, and the OWASP AI Testing Guide v1.0 publication, as corrected). At v1.3.2 the note counted these items among the nine. The ID 18/24 row states that ID 18/24 is the number of the MAS circular to insurers, and that the paper itself carries no reference number. The Project MindForge row names the Phase 1 whitepaper and the three Phase 2 publications, read at the primary. It links the MAS Project MindForge page, gives the dates 2023–2026, and states that the MDDI advisories it names were not read. The Phase 1 whitepaper is \"Emerging Risks and Opportunities of Generative AI for Banks: A Singapore Perspective\" (executive summary released 15 November 2023). The Phase 2 publications are the AI Risk Management Toolkit's Executive Handbook (November 2025), Operationalisation Handbook (January 2026) and Implementation Examples (January 2026).</li>\n<li><strong>CFPB Circular 2023-03.</strong> The CFPB withdrew the circular on 12 May 2025 (90 FR 20084; FR Doc 2025-08286, item 7). The notice says the withdrawal is not necessarily final. The row no longer calls the circular the US conduct anchor for the explainability of AI credit decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>UK.</strong> A dated note is added to the ICO row after the erratum of 12 September 2026. The ICO's plans page gives \"Winter 2026\" for the final Automated Decision Making and Profiling guidance. For that guidance only, the note corrects the erratum's withdrawal of the target. The erratum's limb on the Code of Practice stands. The White Paper row names CP 815 (March 2023) and the government response, CP 1019 (6 February 2024). The Data (Use and Access) Act row gives the commencement provisions of s.80 and Schedule 6. It cites s.142(2)(h) for the commencement of s.80 at Royal Assent for specified purposes, in place of s.142(1)(2)(h). The Bank of England AI Consortium row is read at the four sets of minutes and becomes [VERIFIED-PRIMARY]. Its description says that the minutes record members' concerns, in place of emerging supervisory concerns, and adds the concentration of AI providers. The four sets of minutes are those of May 2025, October 2025, February 2026 and June 2026. The row names model homogeneity and correlated errors across firms, in place of model herding, and two agentic concerns (agentic payments, safe deployment of agentic tools).</li>\n<li><strong>Standards.</strong> ISO/IEC 27090 is an International Standard under publication (stage 60.00), with the publication date 2026-10. ISO/IEC TS 4213:2022 is current and is expected to be replaced by ISO/IEC DIS 4213. EN 18286:2026 is published, by the CEN-CENELEC news item of 31 July 2026; the Official Journal reference is not checked. The rows give the titles of ISO/IEC 27090 and EN 18286:2026, and the TS 4213 row gives the date October 2022, in place of 2022. The EN 18286 row also cites the CEN-CENELEC newsletter item of 29 July 2026, which says that the Commission is expected to publish the reference in the Official Journal later in 2026. The ISO/IEC 27090 row also records the edition that the ISO page shows, Edition 1.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST.</strong> The COSAIS concept paper was released on 14 August 2025, not 26 August, and an annotated outline followed on 8 January 2026. NIST AI 300-1 (ipd) carries its title; the draft asks for input by 16 September 2026, and the row states that the 13 and 15 October dates are not in the draft (15 October 2026 is the comment date of SP 1353). NIST SP 1353 carries \"Using Artificial Intelligence (AI)\" in its title, in place of \"Using AI\". The NIST AI 300-1 row dates the draft July 2026 and states that 16 September 2026 is a date now passed. It also states that the draft says later input may still be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Other rows.</strong> SB 813 (California): the duty-holder is cited at §§8898.1–8898.2; §8898.3 places duties on a designated IVO. IOSCO: OR/07/2026 is a toolkit extract and not a consultation, and the report builds on two earlier IOSCO AI reports. The row dates the extract May 2026 and states that the report includes a survey that was open until 26 June 2026. The row names the two earlier reports, FR06/2021 and CR/01/2025, as the media release IOSCO/MR/13/2026 does. Brazil, PL 2338/2023: the text before the Chamber of Deputies (Art. 14) does not name credit scoring. The Senate and Chamber steps are read at the primary, and \"modelled partly on the EU AI Act\" is marked as secondary. The row dates the Senate approval 10 December 2024 and the referral to the Chamber 17 March 2025, and states that the bill awaits the rapporteur's opinion in the Special Commission. The row cites Art. 14 as items I to XII, and states that item IV covers access to essential private and public services. The row gives the URLs of the Senate page, the Chamber page and the text, in place of the Senate's host name (one place). China: CAC Order No. 15 is dated 10 July 2023 and in force on 15 August 2023, read at the primary. Korea: the PIPC guide was released on 6 August 2025, read at the primary. The row links the guide page (posted 22 August 2025) and its press release, in place of the PIPC home page, and names the last stage \"application and management\". It gives the guide's Korean title and states that the English title is a translation. Colorado: the row quotes the issuer's description of SB 24-205 and SB 26-189. It no longer calls the rules the first comprehensive US state ADMT rules. ECB: the SSM priorities row gives two priorities and an outlined strategy on AI, with the dated URL. Its quotation reads \"digital and, in particular, AI-related strategies\", in place of \"digital (and AI-related) strategies\". The ESAs DORA incidents report and the HKMA circular are given under their issued titles. The ESAs row describes the report as the first annual overview, the 2025 report, and dates its press release 3 June 2026. The EBA follow-up report on machine learning for IRB models carries its identifier, EBA/REP/2023/28. The EU transparency Guidelines pinpoint reads printed p.49 (PDF page 50).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.4 Labs register and verification record</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Labs register rows.</strong> The Responsible Scaling Policy row lists versions v1.0 to v3.4 with their effective dates, and the date on which the page was last updated, 14 August 2026. Its title reads \"(v1.0 to v3.4)\", in place of \"(v2.x updates)\". It gives each version's effective date (v1.0 19 September 2023; v2.0 15 October 2024; v2.1 31 March 2025; v2.2 14 May 2025; v3.0 24 February 2026; v3.1 2 April 2026; v3.2 29 April 2026; v3.3 26 May 2026; v3.4 8 July 2026, current), in place of \"updated Oct 2024–2025\". The agentic misalignment follow-up row describes four further simulated case studies. It names them (covert code sabotage, assisting fraud, motivated mislabeling and coaching human proxies to whistleblow) and says that one sentence of the post notes mitigation progress on the original blackmail evaluations, in place of saying that the post tracks whether mitigations reduced misaligned agentic behaviour. The GPT-6 Astra System Card row lists its amendments of 9, 22 and 29 September 2026. It counts six change-log entries, in place of two, and adds those of 22 September (a correction of the HealthBench values, an Appendix on GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna and a note on updated alignment evaluations) and 29 September (an Appendix on dots). The o1 System Card row states that the card was updated on 5 December 2024, after the o1-preview card, and gives the reported rates (oversight deactivation in 5%, weight exfiltration in 2% of cases). It gives the date of the o1-preview System Card, 12 September 2024, and no longer calls the o1 card the first reasoning-model card. The Amazon framework row records its update of 17 September 2026, and the zero data retention row records the update of 22 September 2026. By that update, Private Safety Processing is rolling out to API customers, with access expanding in phases, and the row no longer limits its citation to 19 August 2026. The \"Not with a Bug, But with a Sticker\" row describes the book, with its full title and subtitle, authors, publisher, ISBN and date (May 2023), links to the publisher's page in place of MITRE ATLAS, and states that MITRE ATLAS is a separate source. The Mistral moderation row describes the hosted API. It gives the API's 9 categories and two endpoints (raw text and conversational), states that the same API powers moderation in Le Chat, and no longer says that the classifier is for deployers of open-weight models. The Cohere row names Cohere Labs and gives Verga et al. as its example. Verga et al. is cited as \"Replacing Judges with Juries: Evaluating LLM Generations with a Panel of Diverse Models\" (Cohere, 29 April 2024), in place of \"LLMs as auto-graders\" critiques. The Gemini row cites the Gemini 3 Pro Frontier Safety Framework Report, dated November 2025, in place of the Gemini 2.5 and Gemini 3 Pro reports, dated 2025. Four rows link to the pages that carry their sources (OpenAI's practices paper, Anthropic's Frontier Red Team, Purple Llama, AWS AI Service Cards). The AWS row is named \"Nova AI Service Cards / responsible AI resources\", in place of \"Nova model cards / responsible AI evaluations\". The Purple Llama row links Meta's announcement of 7 December 2023 and names its successor page. The links of the OpenAI, Anthropic and AWS rows are <code>https://openai.com/index/practices-for-governing-agentic-ai-systems/</code>, <code>https://www.anthropic.com/research/team/frontier-red-team</code> and <code>https://aws.amazon.com/ai/responsible-ai/resources/</code>, in place of <code>https://openai.com/research/practices-for-governing-agentic-ai-systems</code>, <code>https://red.anthropic.com/</code> and <code>https://aws.amazon.com/ai/responsible-ai/</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code>.</strong> The counts table is corrected to the rows. A note counts a row with two resolutions under the first word of its resolution, in place of the resolution that is dominant. It names the six rows that start CONFIRMED and also carry UNVERIFIED (rows 10, 12, 13, 14, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_B.md</code>.</strong> Two lines carry a dated correction: the NIST COSAIS release date, and the reference numbers of the prohibited-practices Guidelines in row 3 of the regulatory register table. Each correction gives the date or numbers of section 1.3, and the July 2026 results stay as written. A third dated correction, under the summary counts, states that the Corrected column sums to 13, not 12, and gives the count of each section's rows by their resolution; the July 2026 counts stay as written (one place).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/QA_LOG_BATCH1.md</code>.</strong> A dated note follows the quotation of the academic register's Willison entry (one place). It states that the entry now reads \"blog\" and that \"widely cited\" was removed in this version.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"2. The opinion layer: assurance level, review confidence and evidence","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Assurance level at this version.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Layer 2) states what the register's ratings support. Two tests are rated ★4, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, and both support CLM-09 only. A Tier 2 or Tier 3 opinion, whose scope covers all nine domains, is therefore given at limited assurance in practice. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> refer to that statement, and the template marks its ★ values as illustrative.</li>\n<li><strong>Review confidence.</strong> The rule is stated once, in <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Final Layer), on the ★1–★5 scale, on which no test is rated ★5. High needs a mean ★ of 3.5 or above and L4–L5 evidence above 70%. Medium needs a mean ★ of 2.5 or above and L3–L5 evidence above 70%. Every other case is Low, including an engagement that runs no test. The mean ★ is taken over the tests run in the engagement, each at the rating its canvas carries, in place of a mean across all questions. The indicator table and the worked example follow the rule. The note under the rule states that High review confidence is reachable only by a scope built around the tests rated ★4, which no tier prescribes. The High wording of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part G) states that no tier-prescribed scope reaches the High band, and no longer cites ★4 ratings. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> no longer state that no engagement reaches High. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part G) refer to the rule and no longer restate other thresholds. <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> shows Medium in its example rows. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 states that the review-confidence mean uses each question's current rating and is not a comparison of ratings across anchors. The current rating is the one published in the version of the standard that the engagement applies.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence levels.</strong> <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> defines L1 to L5 as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §5 does, and <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code> applies the scale by reference to §5. In its model card entry, L4 covers evaluation results produced independently of the developer, in place of results from a published benchmark. In its model evaluation entry, L3 is demonstration, in place of design review, and both entries cite §5. The glossary of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (J.1) states that §5 governs. Independent testing in a staged environment is L4; the maturity of its method is rated on the ★ scale. <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> refers to the tier's evidence minimum.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier 3 evidence.</strong> L5 before the opinion comes from a production-like system or a similar deployment. Where no such L5 exists for a critical claim, the opinion states the gap and is given at limited assurance. Post-deployment operational validation supplies L5 for the first re-assurance (<code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_RES.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusions and gates.</strong> \"Should Proceed\" rests on evidence that meets the tier's evidence minimum (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>). A Not Testable presumption leaves the claim unassessed. For a Critical claim, the presumptive opinion is then Unable to Form Opinion, as <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code> provides where the evidence is insufficient to assess a Critical claim; this replaces Unable to Form Opinion \"on that claim\". The Deployment Opinion phase includes \"Unable to Form Opinion\", and the Major Change Review phase publishes an updated opinion with a new basis date. Gate 4 of <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> now allows a priority question to be scoped out with rationale, requires no test at Tier 0, rates each test that is run, and states the evidence condition from Tier 2: no material claim rests solely on L1–L2 evidence. The core claims are the claims in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>. The templates' instructions take the core claims from that register, and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> asks for each by its register ID and text. The two templates no longer set a range of 5 to 10 core claims (<code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, Part A.1; <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>, Component 3, Part A); the sub-claims of the safety case (Component 6) keep their range.</li>\n<li><strong>★4 form of words.</strong> <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §8 and <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (J.4) no longer say that a ★4 rating is stated without additional caveat (two places). The rating needs no maturity caveat, but a caveat that the test's own canvas requires is still stated. Each ★4 canvas, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, requires one.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"3. Tiering, re-assessment and the lifecycle","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Tier derivation (<code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>).</strong> The score moves in steps of 0.125, and the band table gives each value one tier: 1.000–1.500, 1.625–2.250, 2.375–3.125 and 3.250–4.000. Tier 0 applies only to a system with all five Tier 0 characteristics; a system that scores in the Tier 0 range without them is assigned Tier 1. The tier profiles describe typical systems, and the algorithm assigns the tier.</li>\n<li><strong>Scope.</strong> The Tier 0 profile names its questions, as the Tier 0 scope table does, in place of \"IRQ-GOV series\": IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -005, -009 and -014, at documentary depth. The Tier 1 profile and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5 refer to the Tier 1 scope table. The table takes all GOV questions and priority questions from the Data, Model and Human Oversight domains. It also takes the Resilience and Conduct procedures that the playbook lists for Tier 1. Its critical claims take \"Output confidence is calibrated\" from the list that the profile gave at v1.3.2; the table already held a claim on escalation (\"Escalation pathways work\") and a claim on clean data (\"Data is clean\"), the subjects of that list's two other claims.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-assessment.</strong> Periodic Assurance follows the depth set for each tier (<code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>). In <code>00_Admin/TIERING_MODEL.md</code>, each tier's profile and scope table now give the same re-assessment triggers. The Tier 0 and Tier 1 profiles take the triggers of their tables. Tier 2 adds changed decision logic and new customer segment to its table, and customer segment expansion and operating envelope change to its profile. Tier 3 adds decision logic change and regulatory feedback to its profile, and its table names model change and data change, in place of model version change and training data change. The major-change triggers of <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> apply at every tier, the questions they name are the minimum re-tested within the engagement's scope, and trigger 8, Regulatory Change, is added. <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part H) follows: it requires re-assessment at least of the questions named for the trigger, in place of a full re-assessment. An opinion is valid no later than the next required re-assessment, and annual re-assessment is required at minimum. <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> (Part A.2) states that annual re-assessment is required at minimum, in place of \"recommended\".</li>\n<li><strong>Lifecycle.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code> (Phase 6) uses the eight phases of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> (Layer 4).</li>\n<li><strong>Prioritisation and disclosure.</strong> Phase 3 ranks failure modes by the risk profile, not by a sum or difference of scores, as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §7 forms no composite score. It also notes that on the five-axis scale Detectability 4 is the hardest to detect, a silent failure. <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3c requires a conclusion that rests on a test rated ★3 or below to state the rating and its consequence.</li>\n<li><strong>Versioning and inclusion.</strong> A new subdomain needs maintainer approval and a minor version (<code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §6). <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3a states that a four-test criterion is satisfied when it holds for the deployments to which the question applies. A limit on an answer is stated with the answer and does not make the criterion unmet. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> applies that rule to the four-test records of IRQ-SEC-012 and IRQ-TPR-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Templates and records.</strong> <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> uses questions in its examples that match the claims, and Component 4 lists the seven categories of <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code>. In Component 3, the claim on authorised sources rests on IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-SEC-008 and IRQ-SEC-011, and the claim on escalation rests on IRQ-HUM-004 and IRQ-HUM-005. The two worked examples cite IRQ-MOD-006 (confidence calibration), IRQ-MOD-001 and IRQ-HUM-004, in place of IRQ-MOD-004 (hallucination detection), IRQ-MOD-015 and IRQ-HUM-007. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> states the restructure additions as seven added, net +3 after four merges.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"4. Statements of absence corrected","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-008.</strong> The statement that no peer-reviewed literature addresses operational-memory poisoning is corrected. AgentPoison (NeurIPS 2024) is cited with its limits: it measures attack success, not the validation of memory before reuse. OWASP Agentic AI: Threats and Mitigations (February 2025) is no longer called the first structured articulation of memory poisoning, because AgentPoison (July 2024) is earlier (fields 9 and 17).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-014 and TP-MOD-014.</strong> The statement that no published methodology exists is replaced. Two workshop papers publish repeat-run methods (Atıl et al., 2025; Khatchadourian and Franco, 2025), with their limits. No method specific to financial services was located for the other limbs. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §8 no longer lists TP-MOD-014 as \"None established\". IRQ-MOD-014 field 11 and TP-MOD-014's ★ summary say that test design rests largely on tester judgement, in place of entirely.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-012 and TP-SEC-012.</strong> FinSafetyBench (Findings of ACL 2026) is cited as partial evidence on the refusal limb only. The CFPB report is described as the only verified regulatory anchor. Incident register row 85 is cited as an instance of the mechanism outside financial services, and the gap for financial services is kept (fields 15, 18 and 21).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-007.</strong> King et al. (AIES 2025) narrows the [UNVERIFIED] tag on the consumer-versus-enterprise terms pattern. The tag stays for a firm's own contract terms and deployed tier.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-007.</strong> Kozodoi et al. (European Journal of Operational Research, 2025) is cited as a limit on the reject-inference component.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-012.</strong> Roychowdhury et al. (2024) is cited as a published critique of the RAGAS relevance metrics, so a relevance score is read as a comparator. Reference [6] is added.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"5. Recoverability scores","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Field 16.</strong> Recoverability is scored 3 in place of 2, with the controls-in-place case of field 15 (IRQ-AGT-001, -005, IRQ-CON-001, -002, -008, -009, IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-HUM-001, -002, -003, -004, -006, -007, -008, -009, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-SEC-001, -002, -013, -014, IRQ-TPR-001). Each canvas states recovery within days or longer, except IRQ-SEC-001, which states recovery within hours to days; there the longer period governs. <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §7 scores recovery within hours 2 and recovery within days 3. Where a canvas states weeks or months, 3 is the nearest anchor, because the scale names no band between days and years. IRQ-HUM-004's justification says that the irreversible cases pull towards 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Controls-absent cases (field 15).</strong> IRQ-CON-008 and IRQ-CON-009 score 3 in place of 2, so that the case is not lower than the controls-in-place figure. IRQ-HUM-009, IRQ-SEC-009 and IRQ-MOD-006 (IRQ-MOD-006 in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>) score 4 in place of 3, as §7 scores years-long recovery 4.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>.</strong> The field 15 and field 16 examples follow the same levels. No other axis and no ★ rating changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Lines not changed in this version.</strong> Eight controls-in-place lines in field 15 score 2 and state recovery within hours to days, days, weeks, or weeks to months (IRQ-AGT-004, -013, -014, IRQ-CON-005, -006, IRQ-DAT-002, -003, IRQ-TPR-006). In IRQ-GOV-001 the controls-absent line scores 2 and states recovery within days. Each needs a reading of the whole canvas. They go to the next refresh, with the canvases that score 2 and state no period.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"6. PRA SS1/23 cited as its current version","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>The label.</strong> At 65 places in 48 canvases, \"SS1/23, May 2023\" becomes \"SS1/23, April 2026 (16 April 2026, updating 17 May 2023)\" (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, IRQ-DAT-001, -003, -011, -012, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -009, -010, -011, -012, IRQ-MOD-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, -014, -015, -016, IRQ-RES-005, -006, IRQ-TPR-001, -005). The principles the canvases cite are unchanged. The limbs that give the effective date of 17 May 2024 stay, because paragraph 1.5 of the April 2026 version keeps that date. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference entries, other in-text forms and the register note.</strong> The field 20 reference entry for SS1/23 gives the same label and the year 2026 in 59 canvases (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, IRQ-DAT-001, -003, -005, -007, -011, -012, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, IRQ-MOD-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007, -008, -009, -010, -011, -012, -013, -014, -015, -016, IRQ-RES-005, -006, IRQ-SEC-003, -004, -006, -008, -009, -013, -014, IRQ-TPR-004). Eight in-text citations in two other forms, \"SS1/23 Model Risk Management, May 2023\" and a quoted title with \"May 2023\", take the label too (IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-SEC-001, -002, -003, -005, -013, -014). IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006 are the two canvases of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>. In IRQ-GOV-008 the entry names LIAF01/26 as the amendment, published 23 April 2026. The currency note at the SS1/23 row of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> now says that the canvases cite the April 2026 version, dated 16 April 2026 on its cover and published and effective 23 April 2026. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>No version given.</strong> Eight in-text citations in field 17 gave no version and take the label. Four name the title, \"PRA SS1/23 Model Risk Management\" (IRQ-SEC-004, -007, -008, -009). Four use a short form, such as \"PRA SS1/23, Principle 3\", in a canvas that has no SS1/23 reference entry (IRQ-DAT-004, -006, -008, IRQ-SEC-010). Each canvas's change log records it. Other short-form citations without a version are not changed, because the same canvas gives the version in another place, such as its field 20 entry. IRQ-CON-007 field 17 states in words that SS1/23 remains the current document (verified 29 July 2026), and it is not changed.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"7. Published methods added to research bases","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Methods with their limits.</strong> Field 18 cites a published method for the test, or for part of it, and states its limits; field 20 adds the reference. IRQ-AGT-001 (TheAgentCompany), IRQ-AGT-004 (TraceSafe-Bench), IRQ-AGT-005 (SimpleToolHalluBench), IRQ-AGT-007 (Who&amp;When), IRQ-AGT-009 (POSIX), IRQ-AGT-010 (SOP-Bench; token-based replay for conformance checking), IRQ-DAT-003 (inter-coder agreement, Artstein and Poesio; confident learning, Northcutt et al.), IRQ-DAT-004 (Alaa et al.), IRQ-DAT-006 (BackdoorBench, BackdoorLLM and NIST TrojAI, as related work), IRQ-DAT-013 (three studies of manipulated or poisoned feedback, as related work), IRQ-DAT-014 (Jacobs and Wallach, Measurement and Fairness).</li>\n<li><strong>Supervisory and standards sources.</strong> IRQ-CON-008 (FCA, consumer understanding, 13 March 2026), IRQ-GOV-013 (FCA, complaints and root cause analysis), IRQ-GOV-015 (FTC Policy Statement Regarding Advertising Substantiation, 1984), IRQ-GOV-016 (ECB guide on risk data aggregation and risk reporting, May 2024), IRQ-HUM-005 and IRQ-RES-007 (NIST SP 800-84), IRQ-RES-004 (NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, section 3.5), IRQ-SEC-011 (NIST SP 800-53A Rev. 5), IRQ-HUM-004 (ISO/IEC TS 8200:2024, for its published scope only).</li>\n<li><strong>Further peer-reviewed methods.</strong> IRQ-RES-005 (Rabanser, Günnemann and Lipton, NeurIPS 2019), IRQ-RES-006 (Cerqueira et al., KDD 2026), IRQ-SEC-003 (Open-Prompt-Injection, AgentDojo and PoisonedRAG; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>), IRQ-SEC-009 (MalHug, ASE 2024), IRQ-SEC-014 (Pedro et al., ICSE 2025), IRQ-TPR-001 (Foundation Model Transparency Index, 2023 and 2025; NIST SP 1326), IRQ-TPR-005 (Nikolic et al., NeurIPS 2025), IRQ-TPR-006 (MTEB, EACL 2023; Drift-Adapter, EMNLP 2025).</li>\n<li><strong>Related work only.</strong> IRQ-AGT-012 (ST-WebAgentBench) and IRQ-CON-009 (SAGE) cite related work that is not a method for the test. The statements that the canvases keep stand: IRQ-DAT-013's research-gap flag, IRQ-TPR-001's statement on AI provider due diligence and IRQ-TPR-005's [UNVERIFIED] marker. No ★ rating changes.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"8. Incident register rows 47 to 91","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>.</strong> Rows 47 to 91 are added, each read at the source its Source cell cites. A note at the foot defines the marker forms. A row whose account rests wholly or in part on another kind of source carries a [secondary — source type] marker. This follows the owner's decision of 6 October 2026. Row 64 carries the marker on the owner's decision of 10 October 2026, because its study is read at its abstract, and IRQ-DAT-014 cites it as a secondary row. The primary kinds are a court, a regulator, an official investigation and the affected organisation's own statement. Of rows 47 to 91, four carry a label in their Incident cell. Rows 62 and 91 are non-AI analogues, row 66 is an audit finding of control gaps, and row 56 is a hard-coded rule in an AI underwriting flow. The Institution cell of 21 rows (61, 65 to 70, 72 to 77 and 81 to 88) states that the sector is not financial services. No IRQ star rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion note and legend.</strong> A dated note at the head (7 October 2026) admits an official audit finding of control gaps with no harm event. The condition is that the audit is evidence for a question about the existence of a control. Row 66 is the first such row. The legend names the foot note of 9 October 2026 on rows 47 to 91.</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>.</strong> The register is described as 91 publicly documented incidents. Four are labelled automation or non-AI analogues (rows 2, 14, 62 and 91), and one is an audit finding of control gaps with no harm event (row 66).</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"9. Canvas citations of the new incident rows","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Field 18.</strong> 49 canvases cite rows 47 to 91 in the reserved form (IRQ-AGT-001, -002, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009, -011, IRQ-CON-002, -005, -008, -009, IRQ-DAT-005, -010, -011, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-001, -002, -003, -005, -007, -009, -010, -012, IRQ-HUM-002, -005, IRQ-MOD-001, -005, -011, -014, IRQ-RES-003, -005, -006, IRQ-SEC-002, -003, -005, -008, -009, -010, -011, -014, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -004, -005, -007, -008, -009). IRQ-SEC-012's citation of row 85 is recorded in section 4. Each citation states the row's limits: its setting, a secondary marker, or an allegation that is not adjudicated.</li>\n<li><strong>Statements that stand.</strong> Where a row does not meet a field's statement of absence, the statement stands: IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-DAT-013, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-001 and IRQ-TPR-009. IRQ-CON-002's unverified record is narrowed, not cleared, and IRQ-CON-005's data-sparsity limb stays without incident evidence. No ★ rating changes.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"10. Other corrections","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>TP-CON-001 §6.</strong> A Fail limb is added for a fairness metric selected, changed or substituted after subgroup or intersectional results have been seen, as IRQ-CON-001 field 13 states it (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON_001.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014.</strong> Field 7 and Test 2 name their criterion: harm to uninvolved third parties is recorded in six of the eleven events (events 1–4, 7 and 10). The AISI quotation ends where AISI's sentence ends. The WHEN line dates the OpenAI / Hugging Face compromise to July 2026 on OpenAI's findings of 26 August 2026, added as reference [15]. The INC-043 exclusion cites the system card at p.55, where the passage stands. No change to ★2, any five-axis value or the count of eleven.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-005.</strong> DORA is cited at Art. 6 (ICT risk management framework) and Art. 9 (protection and prevention), in place of Art. 9 alone. Reference [1] names the latest release of the OWASP Machine Learning Security Top 10, v0.3 (pre-release, 29 September 2023).</li>\n<li><strong>Dated notes.</strong> The notes at IRQ-DAT-014, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-TPR-004 and IRQ-TPR-005 field 17 state what their dates cover.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-001.</strong> The MAS FEAT note names PRIN 2A.2 and PRIN 2A.3 to 2A.6, in place of a \"general fair-treatment outcome\", which PRIN 2A does not contain.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-006.</strong> The Vaccaro et al. title reads \"Does What It Wants\", as the article's first page gives it.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-005 and TP-CON-005.</strong> In the ★1 statement, \"among it\" becomes \"including\".</li>\n<li><strong>EBA/GL/2019/02.</strong> The URL points to the Final Report PDF, in place of a page that now shows the EBA third-party risk management guidelines (IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -003, -007, -009; the regulatory register).</li>\n<li><strong>Related IRQs.</strong> IRQ-MOD-002 and IRQ-MOD-008 list IRQ-HUM-001 in field 19 and in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code>, as their failure chains name it.</li>\n<li><strong>Registers.</strong> The labs register's head note states that a row records a check only where the row gives it. It also states that no row of the register's tables carries an [UNVERIFIED] tag. The labs register row on the August 2026 risk report refers to incident register row 32. The row on the Claude Fable 5.1 and Claude Mythos 5.1 system card gives the model name that each cited page uses. The executive summary (p.4) names Mythos 5.1 and §6.2.2 (p.96) names Fable 5.1, which the card (p.2) describes as the same model with different safeguards. The academic register's ReguSim entry states that the search finds the phrases in that entry only. It also states that the search was repeated on 8 October 2026 with the same result. Incident register row 11 adds CNN, 1 June 2023, which carries the post's wording. A dated correction after the academic register's note of 3 October 2026 states that four further rows, not one, record a Crossref check (one place).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §9.</strong> The index points to the files that hold the full procedure sheets, one file for each procedure, in place of the domain files (one place).</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Live Testing (the regulatory register).</strong> Two lines no longer describe live testing in production as part of FAIAS's evidence model. They state that FAIAS's reviewer never tests production systems and point to the Non-Production Rule in <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 (two places).</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>: the version line, the banner and the errata record.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.3, in place of 1.3.2, and the banner gives its date, 9 October 2026, in place of October 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-09.md</code>, as \"v1.3.3, changes to text signed at v1.3.2\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>: the field 11 example.</strong> The example no longer assumes that the staging environment is realistic to production. It states that this is not assumed without a Parity Declaration, as <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> (L3) and <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> state (one place).</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"11. Procedures, observations and further corrections","html":"<h3>11.1 TP-CON-001 and IRQ-CON-001: the perception component</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Untested component.</strong> This rule applies where no subgroup-labelled evaluation set in the component's modality is available, or where no non-production instance of the component is available. The Pass, Fail or Partial result is then reached without the computation of the component's error rates. The component is reported beside the result as a coverage gap, in the report and in the opinion, in the wording of field 14. It does not by itself make the result a Fail, a Partial or Not Testable. This replaces \"a coverage gap, not a pass\" for the perception component (IRQ-CON-001 fields 11, 12 and 14; TP-CON-001 §3, step 13 and §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Fail limb.</strong> The perception-component Fail indicator also covers the firm's own testing that does not report the component's error rates (field 13; TP-CON-001 §6). The grading of these cases changes. ★3 unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.2 TP-RES-007 and IRQ-RES-007: the scenario library</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fail.</strong> The three named scenario types are provider outage, model behavioural drift and agentic misbehaviour. A scenario library that holds none of them is a Fail, whatever other AI-specific scenarios it holds (field 13; TP-RES-007 §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Partial.</strong> The Partial covers a library that holds the synthetic-media scenario and one or two of those three types. It also covers two further cases, with the gap named. In the first, the AI-specific scenarios were exercised only in an earlier review cycle. In the second, a severity assessment assumes diversification that measured concentration does not support (field 14; TP-RES-007 §6).</li>\n<li><strong>Correlated concentration event.</strong> This scenario type is recorded and reported but not graded. The grading of these cases changes. ★3 · P3 unchanged; the assurance objective is unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.3 URO-7 and URO-8 opened</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-7, IRQ-SEC-003.</strong> OWASP LLM01:2025 gives prevention and mitigation strategies, not a test method: its strategy 7 asks for penetration testing and breach simulations without giving a method. The observation asks whether the ★4 and TP-SEC-003's method class (Established Benchmark) hold once that limb is read as mitigation guidance. Flagged 8 October 2026; resolve by 2027-04-08 (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> fields 11 and 18; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_003.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>URO-8, IRQ-SEC-001.</strong> The observation asks the same question for IRQ-SEC-001, whose independent test names LLM01:2025 as its methodology. It asks whether the ★4 and TP-SEC-001's method class (Established Benchmark) hold once that limb is read as mitigation guidance. The question is whether the other sources that the research basis cites carry the ★4 without it. Flagged 8 October 2026; resolve by 2027-04-08 (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>; IRQ-SEC-001 fields 11 and 18; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_001.md</code>). The method-class table of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> marks the classes of TP-SEC-001 and TP-SEC-003 as under review, with their URO numbers (two places).</li>\n<li><strong>The bound.</strong> §6A.2 states: \"Resolution is bounded: the next signing batch or six months, whichever is first.\" It also states: \"The standing ★ is untouched while a URO is open.\" Both ★4 ratings stand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.4 EU AI Act, Articles 13 and 86</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-008.</strong> Article 13 is cited as the duty from provider to deployer that it is, which gives the customer no right of its own. Article 86(1), the right to explanation of individual decision-making, is added with its scope and its application dates, read in the consolidated text of 27 July 2026. The Act gives a right to an explanation, not a right to contest, and field 5 no longer credits it with contestability expectations. Reference [3] names the articles.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-009.</strong> Article 86 is added and is stated as narrower than the canvas's disclosure control. The person exercises the right for a decision already taken. Annex III point 5(b) excludes systems used to detect financial fraud. Reference [8] is added.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-007.</strong> The Article 13 limb says \"deployers\", the term Article 13(1) uses, in place of \"users\". The Independent Tests and the ★ ratings are unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.5 PRA SS1/21 and SS2/21</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Versions.</strong> SS1/21 is cited as its March 2022 version (11 March 2022, updating 29 March 2021) (IRQ-HUM-003, -005, IRQ-RES-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_RES.md</code>; and IRQ-AGT-014, under \"Text citations\" below). SS2/21 is cited as its November 2024 version (15 November 2024, updating 29 March 2021) (IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -006, -008, -009; <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR_009.md</code>, whose change <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> records). In both labels the days are the dates that the PRA's publication page for each statement gives for the current version and for the first publication; the covers give the month. The expectations cited are unchanged. The SS2/21 row of the regulatory register states the currency of the cited passages, including the version published on 18 March 2026 with its renumbered paragraphs. The row also states that the canvases and procedures cite the November 2024 version, in place of the March 2021 text, and what that version changed. That version restated the Solvency II references as PRA Rulebook rules and reworded paragraphs 2.1, 5.3 and 5.4. For the version of March 2026, the row states that it adds notification and register-submission duties for material third party arrangements, extends several Chapter 5 expectations from outsourcing to third party arrangements, and renumbers some cited passages (due diligence from 5.19 to 5.16, concentration from 5.24 to 5.25).</li>\n<li><strong>Reference entries.</strong> The field 20 reference entries give the same versions, with the years 2022 and 2024. SS1/21: IRQ-AGT-002, -005, -006, -012, -013, -014, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-003, -005, IRQ-RES-001, -002, -003, -004, -005, -006, -007. SS2/21: IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, -002, -006, -008, -009. IRQ-GOV-011's entry gives the title as published, \"Outsourcing and third party risk management\". Where an entry also names FCA PS21/3, PS21/3 keeps its own date, 2021. Each canvas's change log records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Text citations.</strong> IRQ-AGT-014 field 17 cites SS1/21 as its March 2022 version and FCA PS21/3 at its own date, 2021, where the citation dated both to 2021. IRQ-GOV-011 fields 9 and 17 give the SS2/21 title as published, \"Outsourcing and third party risk management\".</li>\n<li><strong>Statements corrected to the text.</strong> IRQ-GOV-011: SS2/21 expects firms to keep records of their outsourcing arrangements (para 4.15) and refers to the EBA expectation of a register (para 4.16). The register cross-check is the canvas's extension of those expectations. IRQ-RES-005: SS1/21 states that maintaining operational resilience is a dynamic activity (para 4.16) and states no monitoring expectation. IRQ-RES-006: SS1/21 sets no detection obligation and expects scenario testing to focus on recovery and response (para 6.1). IRQ-RES-003: the limb cites a recoverability expectation, with no containment. IRQ-HUM-005 and IRQ-AGT-002: tested containment is the canvas's reading of the sources, not their requirement; IRQ-HUM-005 field 9 cites PS21/3 for its scenario testing requirement (SYSC 15A.5.3R); IRQ-AGT-002 cites SS1/23 Principle 5.2 for restrictions on model use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.6 Wording, records and citation form</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The maintainer's signing check.</strong> <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2 and §6A.6 and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> state that the maintainer's signing check enforces the resolve-by bound. <code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code> states that the same check enforces the agreement of each register row's Related-IRQs column with its canvas's field 19. The check is a script that the maintainer runs over the standard's files before each signing. It is not published with the standard. These files name the maintainer's signing check where they said \"the corpus check\", \"enforced mechanically\" or \"machine-checked\". In §6A.6, it is the check that reads a capability-envelope note and that fails a note with no out-of-scope limb.</li>\n<li><strong>Working labels.</strong> The titles of the incident register, the academic register, the labs register, the regulatory register and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> no longer carry phase labels. <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_CLAIM_MATRIX.md</code> no longer refer to working phases, and the URO-1 record refers to the maintainer's analysis record (not published). §6A.3 no longer marks the cadence tiers as an assumption. A question once recorded as held for the owner is recorded as an open question (incident register row 34; IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-MOD-002, -009, -015; the academic register).</li>\n<li><strong>CMORG AI Baseline Guidance Review.</strong> It is cited by its cover date, Version 1.0, January 2025, with its publication date, 15 May 2025 (IRQ-RES-001, -007; the regulatory register). The register row states that the served file was created on 14 April 2025, and that an earlier reading recorded May 2025, which the file does not show. The Firm Guidance for Frontier AI row of the regulatory register cites this row as \"AI Baseline Guidance Review, Version 1.0, January 2025\", in place of \"AI Baseline Guidance Review, May 2025\".</li>\n<li><strong>Records.</strong> <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> states that the Control Objective column summarises field 8, which governs. <code>ownership/OWNERSHIP_PROOF.md</code> gives the replaced v1.1.1 signing as dated 26 August 2026 and made on 27 August. The incident register's foot note says that rows 27 to 31 were added on the same day as that signing, in place of the day after. IRQ-SEC-001 gives the number of incidents it cites, two (DPD and Chevrolet of Watsonville), in place of \"repeatedly\". <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code> allows the primary to follow later in the same incident citation and defines <code>YEAR</code> as the year, stated in the row's Date cell, of the event or report on which the citing field relies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>11.7 URO-6 at its signing-batch limit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-6, IRQ-DAT-009.</strong> This release is a signing batch, so URO-6 passed its signing-batch limit unresolved. It stays open to its date bound of 3 April 2027, and its ★3 stands. A note in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> records this, in the form of the note of 3 October 2026 on URO-2 to URO-5.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"12. AI trust view","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TRUST_VIEW.md</code> (new).</strong> The page is an index. It lists the questions of the Governance, Security and Operational Resilience domains and the claims of the Security, Resilience and Governance themes. It adds no question or claim, and no rating changes. It carries no ★ rating or test status. Each row repeats the text of <code>IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> or <code>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>. Where a row and its register differ, the register governs. FAIAS has no \"trust\" domain: under the homing rule, each question keeps its one domain.</li>\n<li><strong>Pointers.</strong> <code>README.md</code> §1 (after the domain table) and §2 (the file map and the reading order), <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TAXONOMY.md</code> (after the homing rule) and <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §1 (after the fundamental posture) point to the page.</li>\n</ul>"}]};
